{"id":"live-dead","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a0","title":"Live view doesn't work at all — and e2e doesn't catch it","done":"2026-08-09T20:34:19-04:00","desc":"FIXED via kolu PR #2133 (the store merge stops guessing keys) plus PR #61 (pin bump and the failing-shape e2e scenarios: mid-insert, mid-delete, reorder, poisoning — asserting the id multiset).\n- Observed 2026-08-09 in real use: a write to the served directory never reaches the page without a manual reload, while the e2e liveness suite stays green\n\n**DIAGNOSED 2026-08-09** (Grok; DIAGNOSIS.md in the live-dead worktree).\n- Root cause is UPSTREAM in kolu: @kolu/surface's solid writeWrappedValue merges each stream frame with Solid reconcile(next) under the DEFAULT key 'id' — but olai's outline nodes are Located records ({file,line,node:{id,…}}) with no top-level id, so every element keys as undefined and any membership/order change mis-merges the client store: missing nodes, duplicated nodes, scrambled lines, and a poisoned store that stays wrong for all later frames until reload\n- Server, watcher, probe, bind and WS frames are all healthy (verified leg by leg; the correct frame reaches the browser)\n- A/B-verified: reconcile(next,{key:null}) or plain replacement fixes mid-insert/mid-delete/reorder\n\n**Why e2e is green:** every liveness scenario edits in exactly the shapes keyed-reconcile survives — whole-file rewrites at stable indices and end-of-file appends — never a mid-insert, mid-delete, or reorder, and never an assertion on the full id multiset.\n\n**Fix direction:** upstream PR to kolu (writeWrappedValue must not assume key 'id' for arbitrary payloads — caller-supplied key or replace policy from the stream definition), plus e2e scenarios that fail today: mid-insert, mid-delete, reorder, mid-insert-then-append poisoning, asserting the id multiset rather than eventual title text.\n\n**Upstream fix in flight 2026-08-09:** folded into the kolu-side agent's scope (branch surface-typed-lifecycle), pointed at DIAGNOSIS.md.\n- Olai still owes, once the kolu pin moves: the failing-today e2e scenarios and the pin bump","after":["live"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"no-lifecycle","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a1","title":"Stale server accepted silently — no connection indicator","done":"2026-08-09T20:34:19-04:00","desc":"Diagnosed 2026-08-09.\n- Surface is designed to address exactly this class of bug and olai doesn't use that part: packages/web/src/client/wire.ts calls connectSurface (the no-lifecycle seam) and keeps only .client, discarding the wire status accessor\n- A tab wired to a replaced/dead server just sits there looking fine: on reconnect the new process closes it with the stale-tab code, the wire retires by design (stops re-dialing, reports status 'retired'), and nothing in the UI says so\n- **Fix:** make full use of the framework — derive createServerLifecycle (or consume the status connectSurface already returns) and render a GREEN CONNECTION INDICATOR in the UI (kolu's header dot is the house pattern), with the retired state auto-reloading or showing 'server restarted — reload'\n- **Regression test (HACKING.md):** restart the scenario-owned server under an open page and assert the page recovers; assert the indicator reflects connected/disconnected/retired\n- Pairs with the connection-health pill under mobile-pwa","after":["live"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"no-lifecycle-fix","parent":"no-lifecycle","ord":"a0","title":"The fix in olai","done":"2026-08-09T20:34:19-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #61 (squash commit 46b3a70), rebuilt on top of kolu PR #2133 before it merged. What it added:\n- A connection indicator: a dot in the corner, green only while the page is really talking to a live server.\n- Recovery when the connection has given up reconnecting: the page says \"server restarted — Reload\" and reloads through reloadForUpdate.\n- The page derives its connection state with createServerLifecycle, reading the status value the connection already hands back (that status reports a given-up connection as \"down\").\n- A new identity.info member on the surface carries the server's process id to the page, so the page can tell a new server from the one it was talking to. The agent proved the check this replaced had never actually been able to fail.\n- End-to-end tests that restart the server on the same port while a page is open.\n\n**Decided 2026-08-09:** this same PR will also adopt the upstream kolu API described in no-lifecycle-upstream, once that exists.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"no-lifecycle-upstream","parent":"no-lifecycle","ord":"a1","title":"Upstream: make the framework prevent this #upstream","done":"2026-08-09T20:31:33-04:00","desc":"Shipped as kolu PR #2133. What it changed in the framework:\n- The framework now runs the whole stale-tab handshake itself: the system/identity data carries the server's process id, the socket feeds that id back to itself, and olai's own rejectStaleProcess check was deleted.\n- Handling the state where the connection has given up reconnecting is now a required option on connectSurface — the types refuse code that ignores it.\n- The client-side store merge no longer guesses which field identifies a list element from its name.\n- The agent was briefed on three points: making the option required at the type level, the socket feeding its own process-id echo, and the fact that the status projection reports a given-up connection as \"down\", collapsing two different states into one.\n\n**What to PR:** changes to @kolu/surface and surface-app, so a future app cannot silently ship a connection that is dead with nothing saying so. To be worked out from the PR that comes out of no-lifecycle-fix (decided 2026-08-09).\n\n**Preference (the human, 2026-08-09):** enforcing it in the types is always best — the API should require the caller to consume the status/onRetired value instead of letting it be thrown away.\n\nOther designs on the table: connectSurface supplying a default handler for the given-up state (reloading via reloadForUpdate) unless the caller overrides it; the app shell (surfaceAppLayer) drawing a built-in connection indicator itself.\n\nContext: docs/brainstorming/surface-utilization.md","after":["no-lifecycle-fix"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"live-dead-residual","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a2","title":"Live update still duplicates rows — one layer above the fixed merge","done":"2026-08-10T13:11:19-04:00","desc":"FIXED 2026-08-10, shipped as PR #70 (squash 298c30c): the client keeps no store of its own.\n\n**DIAGNOSED 2026-08-10**, and it is olai's, one layer above kolu's merge exactly as suspected — but a CONTRACT violation rather than a keying accident.\n- App.tsx reconciled the day's groups (key `file`) and the outline's rows (key `key`) into stores of its own, and what it handed `reconcile` was BORROWED: `situate`/`rowsFor` mint fresh wrappers, but the records inside them are the served set's own Located objects (derive.ts, dates.ts), and `reconcile` unwraps proxies and mutates the previous objects transitively\n- Read two days in turn and the entries inside a group merge POSITIONALLY (a Situated carries no `file`), while the `shows` records either side of that merge do carry one and it matches — so instead of swapping a reference, reconcile copied one node's id/title/date/desc over another node's record, in the live store, which is the set every other page is read from\n- Those writes then invalidated the very memo they came from (`derived` reads every node), so the effect re-triggered itself until the stack ran out: `RangeError: Maximum call stack size exceeded`, frames alternating runUpdates ⇄ completeUpdates\n- Observed in the browser on opening a second day; the Dropbox-port sighting above (a node drawn twice, a day missing from the calendar) is the same class\n- Reproduced headlessly against docs/roadmap.jsonl before any fix — the first navigation never converges\n\n**THE FIX:** no store above the framework's.\n- The rows are a memo, a day's groups are read straight off the page model, and identity across frames — the only thing the stores were for — is a KEYING question, answered where the rows are drawn by `<Key>` (@solid-primitives/keyed) on `Row.key` and on `file/id` for a day entry\n- Nothing in the client writes to what the wire hands it, which is now the rule architecture.md states\n\n**E2E:**\n- `Two days, read in turn, each still says what it said` fails on the old client (day 06 lists `pack` twice) and passes on the new one\n- `An outline that arrives beside the open one leaves it whole` closes the new-files gap this entry named — it is green on the old client too, so that gap was coverage rather than a second bug","after":["live"],"see":["live-dead","solid-utilization"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"sigint-hang","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a3","title":"Ctrl+C doesn't stop `just run`","done":"2026-08-10T12:12:41-04:00","desc":"Done-marked 2026-08-10 (human-confirmed): the fix and its regression test shipped inside chat's PR #63 (squash 66d64ff) — this stamp carries that merge instant.\n- Observed 2026-08-10 on the chat branch; a second wedged instance (a store-only smoke server from the previous night) suggests it predates chat\n- **Investigation so far:** SIGINT is delivered and caught (runMain's handler runs), the listener fully closes (no listening socket, no child processes, no TCP connections remain), yet the process never exits — wedged inside Effect scope teardown after all I/O is gone; repeated Ctrl+C is ignored because the handler already tripped\n- **ROOT CAUSE (fixed in PR #63, with the orchestrator's earlier chat-introduced attribution corrected):** NOT chat-specific — master hangs identically whenever a browser tab is connected (the chat agent reproduced it with a master build; the orchestrator's clean master test was an idle server with no tab, the wrong discriminator)\n- server.close waits for every connection, and a tab holds two that never end on their own — the surface websocket and the page's keep-alive — so a server with a tab attached never finished its listener finalizer\n- **Fix:** the teardown now terminates websockets and destroys idle keep-alives before close\n- **Regression test** packages/server/src/shutdown.test.ts: real child process, real websocket, SIGINT, assert exit within a bound — fails against the old finalizer\n- Fix must include a regression test: start the real server, SIGINT it, assert exit within a bound","after":["live"],"see":["serve-surface-app"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"frame-cap","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a4","title":"The server's websocket message-size limit disagrees with the framework's #techdebt","done":"2026-08-10T14:09:23-04:00","desc":"FIXED 2026-08-10, shipped as PR #71 (squash 3822a29): the cap is sourced from the framework.\n- `maxPayload: RPC_MAX_FRAME_BYTES` + the ndjson delimiter (the newline rides the wire but is outside what the decoder measures), so the two layers can no longer disagree whatever either of them is re-pinned to\n- **Correction to the audit's account, measured while fixing it:** under bun — what actually runs this server — `maxPayload` was never enforced at all. Bun's built-in `ws` ignores the option and applies its own 16 MiB ceiling, closing 1006 \"Received too big message\" (16777216 delivered, one byte more killed)\n- So the 8 MiB cap was inert in production and the raw-1009 death was a node host's failure mode; the misconfiguration was real, its symptom was not reachable here\n- That is why the regression fence (`packages/server/src/listener.test.ts`) pins the NUMBER — it fails against the old 8 MiB and against any future drift — beside an end-to-end test that a 9 MiB frame is carried and answered\n- Also newly known: while bun's ceiling stands, the framework's handled INBOUND oversize path is unreachable here at all — a frame the decoder would refuse is already past bun's limit, and even one at exactly the cap arrives a delimiter byte over it and dies at 1006 rather than 1009\n- **Stale:** superseded 2026-08-10 by PR #78, which moved the cap inside serveSurfaceApp as an unspellable framework constant — see serve-surface-app\n\n**Found by the surface-utilization audit (2026-08-09):** listener.ts sets ws maxPayload to 8 MiB while @kolu/surface's frameLimit classifies oversize at 16 MiB — an 8–16 MiB frame (a big outline snapshot) dies at the raw ws layer with close 1009, taking the whole socket down, instead of the framework's handled oversize path.\n- **Fix:** source the cap from the framework's constant, with a test\n- Audit: docs/brainstorming/surface-utilization.md","after":["live"],"see":["serve-surface-app"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"mcp-fail-visible","parent":"bug-mcp","ord":"a0","title":"A failed MCP server is invisible in the UI","done":"2026-08-13T13:51:08-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 after an incident where the kolu MCP server was not detected. An MCP server that fails to attach to a chat session must be VISIBLE.\n- **The human's position:** MCP must NOT fail silently.\n\n**Shipped as PR #140** (squash 1c56f90), 2026-08-13.\n- A strip under the chat panel header says what happened, in the failing server's own words: refused the identity read / closed without answering / did not answer within 5s / could not be started. It reuses master's existing three-surface vocabulary, shows one alarm dot, and shows nothing at all on a healthy session.\n- One probe answers both halves of the question — which server answered (serverOf) and which is missing (missingFrom). A `servers` event is written to the existing chat data cell before every session opens, and `MissingServer.where` names the binary that was probed.\n- \"Never installed\" (no kolu on PATH and no PADI_SOCKET set) is deliberately not counted as a failure. But PADI_SOCKET set while kolu is absent from the process's PATH is a failure, with its own sentence — the main point left open by the review, closed in the follow-up round of fixes.\n- **The `/simplify` pass found and fixed a live crash in a neighbouring file:** a bad OLAI_ACP_AGENT value dumped an uncaught file-not-found (ENOENT) stack trace and then blamed `initialize`. The \"could not start the program\" handling moved into pipes.ts, and both places that start a subprocess now race the start against an `unstartable` result.\n- **Review:** Grok found zero must-fix issues; all six nice-to-have findings were taken in the follow-up round (the deadline test now costs 150ms rather than 5s; the @phone scenario asserts the strip; the first nice-to-have's test caught the suite accidentally reading this machine's own PADI_SOCKET).\n- The follow-up round was approved; CI 10/10 green on the kolu-ci-1 runner at commit 16b8be5.\n- The end-to-end job again hung for about 4m42s on the ci-2 and ci-4 runners, on two commits; the reviewer judged that a fault of those machines rather than the code — this time the failed logs were kept.","see":["kolu-detect-primary"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"load-perf","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a5","title":"Roadmap initial load feels very slow","done":"2026-08-12T08:24:51-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the human. Symptom: initial load of the roadmap in the web UI is subjectively very slow.\n- Dispatched as measurement + root-cause analysis FIRST — phase breakdown (server load/validate/derive, wire payload, client bundle/render) with hard numbers and a named dominant term; the fix is a separate dispatch after the human reads the RCA\n\n**P0 shipped 2026-08-11:** PR #93 (squash f079b7b).\n- build-time .br/.gz siblings for /assets/*, negotiated by @kolu/surface-app's existing static layer\n- 977 KB → ~253 KB brotli on the wire, browser-verified\n- The RCA and measurement harness were dropped from the PR by the human's own hand (they live in the PR history at 624ffba if ever wanted)\n- P2 shipped separately as snapshot-scale (PR #95)\n- **OPEN:** P1 — code-split markdown/highlight off the critical path (the remaining long-task cost) — undecided, awaiting the human","changed":"2026-08-24T16:02:11-04:00","custom":{"p1-postscript":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: the desc's closing \"OPEN: P1 — awaiting the human\" paragraph is stale — P1 shipped as #118 (59e4192, its own child load-perf-p1, done 2026-08-12); the surviving residual is markdown-raw-flash (this file), which is P1's known cost"}}
{"id":"snapshot-scale","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a6","title":"First snapshot carries every document body — must scale to 1000s of .md files","done":"2026-08-11T11:50:45-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11, escalated by the human from load-perf P2: the manifest cell in the FIRST websocket snapshot inlines the full text of every .md document in the served set (~124 KB of the ~212 KB snapshot for docs/, measured in LOAD-PERF-RCA.md).\n- **The human's requirement, verbatim intent:** olai must support directories with THOUSANDS of .md files — a wire that ships every body up front is O(corpus) on first paint and therefore not acceptable, regardless of perf feel today\n\n**Shape:** documents become a collection on the surface — the first snapshot carries only what the sidebar draws (path, title); a body travels when a document is opened (lazy fetch or per-document subscription).\n- This is a WIRE change only: the server still loads and validates the whole set (format.md's rule — doc references must be checkable — is untouched)\n\n**Interactions:**\n- dovetails with surface-mcp-viewing — kolu surface-mcp projects collections as templated resources, so the documents-as-collection shape serves browser and agents alike; the viewing-slice design (terminal d5df9f63, in flight at filing) must anticipate this shape rather than exposing today's body-carrying manifest cell\n- At 1000s of files the server-side whole-set revalidation cost also deserves its own measurement before it surprises anyone — noted here, not scoped here","see":["load-perf","surface-mcp-viewing"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"mirror-status","parent":"bug-tasks","ord":"a0","title":"A mirror does not reflect its target's mark","done":"2026-08-11T13:01:24-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-11 by the human, seen on the live roadmap.\n- **Bug seen:** a mirror of a node whose target carries doing draws a plain bullet (no checkbox), where the target itself draws the half-filled box\n- Screenshot-confirmed on the \"Markdown rendering, perfected\" pair\n- **Pointer for the fix:** packages/format/src/derive.ts's `Derived.status` doc comment PROMISES mirror coverage (\"a mirror standing for whatever its target stores, since that is what it shows\")\n  - Either the #90 rewrite (stored-only status) dropped the mirror-follows-target hop from the map builder while keeping the comment\n  - Or the row/checkbox path looks the status up under the mirror's own id instead of what it shows\n  - Same question for the blocked index and progressOf: a mirror must ask everything of its TARGET\n- Fix per HACKING.md ships with a test that reproduces: mirror of a doing node shows doing; of a todo node shows todo incl. the empty box; of a done node hides under done-hidden with its subtree ONLY per the done rules; bullet target still shows nothing\n\n**Diagnosed 2026-08-11** (terminal `a1f792c9`, PR #96):\n- NOT a mirror bug — mirrors reflect their target's mark correctly\n- PR #96 (+90/−0, tests+docs) locks that promise at both layers so it cannot silently break: every mark through the rows, both halves of the blocked glyph on a mirror row, done-hidden on a mirror-of-done, and the format.md paragraph (a mirror asks everything of what it shows; a see inherits none of it)\n\n**The screenshot's real cause:**\n- Now entries are regular nodes carrying see (the Now convention dropped real mirrors 2026-08-10 at 849eb9b, because a mirror's four fields cannot hold the terminal/stage/PR prose)\n- A markless node draws a bullet — correct under stored-only status\n- **OPEN, a roadmap-convention call for the human:** should Now entries reflect their item's mark?\n  - (a) back to real mirrors — marks reflect for free, but the prose must live elsewhere\n  - (b) keep see-nodes, status stays a hashtag in the title (today)\n  - (c) mark the Now entries by hand — legal under stored-only, but a second copy to keep in sync\n\n**Shipped 2026-08-11 as PR #96** (squash d4c7df2): the test-lock at both layers plus the format.md paragraph.\n- The Now convention itself was fixed the same day: Now entries are real mirrors again, with the tracking prose on the items\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"git-invisible","parent":"bug-git","ord":"a0","title":"Git state is invisible in the UI #human","done":"2026-08-11T19:31:05-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #108** (squash 70f21dd; done carries the merge instant). Final head f3d0a53:\n- Review approve zero must-fixes, three nits taken\n- Applied.why + the Ops.git cell + the header readout (quiet/no-repo/error-with-reason)\n- Reconciled past #106\n- 20 odu nodes green both platforms\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 by the human, hit live on his vault instance.\n- **Bug:** writes through the agent reported `committed: false` with NOTHING in the UI saying so, on a directory he knows is a git repo\n- By design (ops/src/git.ts) the auto-commit cannot fail the write and collapses every cause — no work tree, git missing from the service PATH, staging/commit refusal, identity unset — into the same quiet flag, with the reason only in the server log\n- **The human's ruling, verbatim intent:** \"even if git detection fails I'd expect it to surface in UI — even just 'Not a Git repo' or 'Git error'.\" Errors may still be IGNORED (never block or fail the write); they must be VISIBLE\n- **Shape:** the server knows workTree and each write's committed + git's said — surface a git status affordance in the app chrome (header, near the live dot) showing repo/no-repo/git-error, and let a write's committed: false say why where the reader can see it\n\n**Dispatched 2026-08-11** -> Kolu terminal `b0ff13b8` (Claude Opus, YOLO; worktree .worktrees/git-invisible; brief at .worktrees/git-invisible-BRIEF.md).\n- #stage review — PR #108 (https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/108) open: the boolean becomes values\n  - Applied.why (one sentence on any uncommitted write's reply, additive)\n  - An Ops.git cell (off/repo/none/error + git's words, probed once per serve, republished on refusal and recovery), drawn as a header readout beside the connection pill (quiet healthy, calm no-repo, error with tip)\n- Classification honesty: nothing-to-commit is never a fault; broken-git is error unless git itself said not-a-repository (LC_ALL=C)\n- Non-blocking guarantee kept\n- 572 unit + 185 e2e green locally; three-state evidence in .worktrees/git-invisible-out/\n\n**REVIEW DONE** (Grok, split 0c913c2d, 2026-08-11): APPROVE, zero must-fixes.\n- Non-blocking guarantee holds, three UI states reproduced independently, classification honest, purity clean\n- Three nits: dead code field, isolate onGit from write failure, e2e for Applied.why\n- #stage finishing: nits + master merge past #106 + odu CI; then FIELDED to the human — he decides the merge\n- Reviewer: Grok (cross-model)\n- **Also now a HACKING.md rule** (f2bbd28, the human's own edit): errors are never silently ignored — they surface in the UX\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"silent-errors","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a8","title":"Silently ignored errors, inventoried and surfaced #human","done":"2026-08-11T22:34:22-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #112** (squash c5774a7), merged automatically by the orchestrator at 2026-08-12T02:34:04Z under the human's advance approval. He waived the evidence requirement by his own word; the gate was: review findings addressed, 18/18 checks green at the real head commit 77c73b6, and no unintended change to the roadmap file.\n- The third merge made without waking the human that night.\n- The agent kept its own gate honest: it noticed that PR #117 landing had made its earlier green result stale, re-merged, and told the orchestrator NOT to act on the stale green.\n\n**Every quietly swallowed error was inventoried and made visible:**\n- A cancel that cannot be honoured now refuses honestly (the grace period was tightened to 5 seconds after review).\n- Stream health is drawn beside the connection indicator.\n- A failure in the storage platform is now data, and the banner shows the REASON.\n- Kolu's failure reasons are carried through, for use by mcp-fail-visible.\n- An image that cannot be resolved names itself.\n- A clipboard permission denial is visible.\n- A refused session list says it was refused.\n- Agent output chunks that are not text are drawn as markers.\n- The optional call `publish?.` now throws instead of quietly doing nothing.\n- Preferences warn once when they cannot be read.\n- **Review:** all 12 review dimensions passed; the findings were hygiene plus the question of the cancel grace period, all addressed.\n- The `/simplify` pass caught two real leaks along the way (absolute paths exposed to the client; an identical frame rebroadcast every 60s) — both now have regression tests.\n\n**Filed and dispatched 2026-08-11** on the human's ask (a paste that did nothing; the audit that followed found the three reverted PRs — see docs/RCA/), to kolu terminal `c65479b2` (Claude Opus, running unattended; reviewer Grok in a split terminal, `b9daf69c`).\n- Part 1, restoring paste, was split out and shipped as PR #111.","see":["git-invisible","mcp-fail-visible","chat"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"paste-restore","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a8","title":"Pasting an image is gone — undo commit dd34849, which removed it under a misleading commit message","done":"2026-08-11","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #111** (squash 2de0ef5), merged BY THE HUMAN directly at 2026-08-11T19:42:50-04:00 (this done stamp is the ops-layer instant, ~minutes after; review and refactor waived by the human).\n- Grok cherry-picked dc4c078, verified the restored e2e coverage, odu green both platforms\n\n**Filed & Dispatched 2026-08-11** (split from silent-errors at the human's direction: separate quick PR) -> Kolu terminal `b3e45fe0` (Grok, always-approve; worktree .worktrees/paste-restore).\n- Cherry-picks dc4c078 (the completed revert-of-dd34849, authored by the silent-errors Opus)\n- Verifies the RESTORED e2e coverage passes (the tests dd34849 deleted are the regression fence that was missing)\n- odu CI both platforms, PR against master\n- **Process, set by the human:** NO review, NO refactor pass — quick; orchestrator fields the PR at green\n","see":["paste-image","silent-errors"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"restore-84","parent":"bug-outline","ord":"a0","title":"Markdown inside titles is gone — undo commit a56cb65, which removed it under a misleading commit message","done":"2026-08-11T20:28:17-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #113** (squash ae59f9e), merged BY THE HUMAN directly at 2026-08-12T00:27:53Z (this done stamp is the ops instant just after; review and refactor waived per the quick-restore process).\n- Inline markdown in titles is back on master, reconciled to coexist with #102's tag pills in one title\n- #84's restored tests pass beside #102's\n\n**Filed & Dispatched 2026-08-11** -> Kolu terminal `c7118f67` (Grok, always-approve).\n- **Origin:** the orchestrator's stamp-commit audit found a56cb65 ('roadmap: title-markdown done (#84)', seconds after the #84 merge) to be the exact content-inverse of squash e1ab412 outside docs/roadmap.jsonl\n- The third mass-revert stamp from the same morning session (with dd34849/#87 and 12ea294/#83)\n- Inline title markdown had been absent from master ever since, while the ledger read done\n","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"sticky-header","parent":"bug-nav","ord":"a0","title":"Header scrolls away — must be sticky","done":"2026-08-11T21:35:40-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #115** (squash df67730), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T01:35:17Z under the human's conditional pre-approval.\n- The visual gate passed on inspection (desktop scrolled deep with the header pinned; phone drawer from a scrolled page still meeting the header seam)\n- Review: approve, zero must-fixes (Grok drove both viewports, spot-checked the diagnostic e2e by reverting the fix)\n- 18/18 required checks green at f83a527; one darwin FSEvents flake in an untouched package flagged honestly, rerun, passed\n- 7 diagnostic e2e each proven failing on the pre-change client\n- **Deliberately untouched:** the desktop sidebar still scrolls with the page — filed as sidebar-sticky for the human's ratification\n\n**Filed & Dispatched 2026-08-11** by the human (screen recording; it showed the page at rest, so the author reproduced the scroll-away itself — header at y=-4963 — before fixing) -> Kolu terminal `b2b467d6` (Claude Opus, YOLO).\n- Interacting chrome kept true: #104's drawer/scrim/sheet anchor at `top-[var(--height-header)]`, contents jumps land clear of the bar (scroll-padding)\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"commit-whole-repo","parent":"bug-git","ord":"a1","title":"Commit UI is blind to edits outside the ops path — .md edits don't show as pending","done":"2026-08-12T13:26:42-04:00","desc":"**Shipped in PR #122** (squash 518f4af, merged 2026-08-12T17:25:56Z; the human approved it and merged it himself). Everything he ratified at dispatch landed.\n\n**Phase one — the refactor:**\n- git.ts moved out into its own leaf package, @olai/git, with one dependency (effect).\n- The commit-message audit convention is passed in as an argument, and git's trailer is returned word for word for the ops layer to classify.\n- The RepoState and Reason types are owned by the git package; @olai/format re-exports them on a `./state` subpath so browser code never reaches `node:child_process`.\n- Every subtlety of the original plumbing was preserved.\n\n**The feature:**\n- The pending-changes view grew four groups: outlines, other files, files inside the served directory, and unpushed commits.\n- The survey now runs from the repository root, so a modified README.md at the root shows up while olai is serving docs/.\n- Changed files that are not outlines appear as one row per path, with a kind chip derived from git's porcelain status output; untracked files are included and .gitignore is respected.\n- Committing a selection commits NAMED PATHS, never the index — work you staged by hand is untouched, and a path with nothing waiting is refused by name.\n- Push arrived on both faces (an Ops.push operation, an MCP push tool, a button in the panel, and a count on the header pill from the same single survey). Refusals are shown word for word, and a push blocked because a rebase is needed says so by name, like commit does.\n- `--commit=auto` is unchanged.\n\n**Review (Grok, two passes) caught two real must-fix bugs:**\n- A refused commit had left its `git add` behind in the real index (fixed — the index is now bit-identical after a failure, proven by a test built from the reviewer's own repro using commit signing).\n- The git package's README was stale.\n- `--no-gpg-sign` is deliberately refused: a signature is the owner's statement, so gpg's refusal is carried through word for word instead.\n\n**Also fixed from review:**\n- The header pill and the panel now use one rule for what counts as waiting (an outline that was only reformatted, with no node changes, counts).\n- A failure of `git status` surfaces as the unusable/git-error face.\n- The documentation was folded into PR #123's docs/git.md.\n- A separate problem found along the way was filed on its own: mcp-stdout-pollution.\n\n- 914 unit tests and 261 end-to-end tests at review time; CI green on both platforms.\n- **Follow-up for the orchestrator:** reconnect the olai MCP server to pick up the push tool and the commit-by-paths argument — then the orchestrator's last direct git command (`git push`) can retire.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"one-git-indicator","parent":"bug-git","ord":"a2","title":"Two git indicators in the header — there should be one","done":"2026-08-11T22:54:54-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #119** (squash b1ca909), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T02:54:36Z under the overnight pre-approval — fourth autonomous merge.\n- **Gate record:** the review's DO-NOT-MERGE round did its job (the 390pt shrink order had traded one wrap bug for another — the CONNECTION pill went illegible)\n- The fix round resolved it and the orchestrator's visual gate confirmed on the refreshed 40-image evidence: \"● live\" fully legible at 390pt both themes, the commit pill the graceful truncator (glyph intact), exactly one git indicator in every shot, verified in the pinned bar\n- 22 odu checks green at e045c73 both platforms; roadmap zero-diff; conflicts with #112 resolved keeping both sides\n- The one-indicator claim is now a control-shaped e2e assertion\n\nFiled 2026-08-11 by the human (screenshot: \"● git\" beside \"✓ committed · 3m ago\").\n- **Dispatched** (overnight slate) -> Kolu terminal `7184295a` (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split `a18ec355`)\n- Bonus: fixed a pre-existing 390pt wordmark-crush bug found while shooting evidence\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"load-perf-p1","parent":"load-perf","ord":"a0","title":"P1: markdown/highlight off the critical path","done":"2026-08-12T08:24:20-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED as PR #118** (squash 59e4192), AUTO-MERGED by the orchestrator 2026-08-12T12:23:45Z under the overnight pre-approval — fifth and final autonomous merge of the slate.\n- **Gate record:** review zero must-fixes with the reviewer running its OWN adversarial network/remount script (outline pages fetch no markdown chunk; markdown-bearing pages fetch once; no remount on a held document)\n- Numbers verified honest and reproducible: document words-on-screen −30.8% (203.7→140.9ms), fully-rendered +0–35ms disclosed as run-variance, main chunk byte-for-byte reproducible at the head\n- 18/18 checks both platforms at f44297e; roadmap zero-diff\n- The upstream ask lives as splitting-upstream (filed by the orchestrator)\n\n**Dispatched 2026-08-11** (overnight slate) -> Kolu terminal `4a78220a` (Claude Opus, YOLO; reviewer Grok, split `43698764`)\n","changed":"2026-08-19T19:13:49-04:00"}
{"id":"sidebar-sticky","parent":"bug-nav","ord":"a1","title":"Desktop sidebar scrolls away with the page — should it pin like the header?","done":"2026-08-12T11:21:36-04:00","desc":"**Shipped in PR #121** (squash 37648ee, merged 2026-08-12T15:20:50Z) — the human's first same-day pre-approved auto-merge.\n- **Full pipeline:** Grok adversarial review: NO must-fix; odu CI 20/20 both platforms at 219d4ae\n- Then the orchestrator's visual gate on the evidence: before — empty column at deep scroll; after — calendar+tree pinned under the header, collapsed rail pinned, column's own scroll independent of the page, phone shots sha256-identical before/after\n- **What shipped:** desktop sidebar (calendar + file tree + icon rail) pins below the #115 header with its own scroll region\n- #105 collapsed-by-default and ⌘\\ survive; mobile drawer untouched; #102 styling not regressed\n- **One non-CSS change:** scroll.ts gained a bounded frame-by-frame clamp-restore retry (1s / takeover / position-holds) because the page-tall column had been quietly propping up document height — regression fence proven failing without it (zoom_and_navigate 1px short)\n- Review corrected a false PR-body claim (3 'pre-existing' bun failures — actually 878/0 green both sides)\n- **Known residuals, recorded as decisions in the PR:** SETTLE_MS=1000 doesn't close the slow-/media/* restore case (predates the pin); the collapse chevron overlays a scrolled tree's foot (#105's control, newly visible)\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"precompress-dev-tax","parent":"bug-build","ord":"a0","title":"Precompression taxes every dev-server start ~20s — recompresses a never-pruned dist","done":"2026-08-12T21:28:58-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 from the human hitting it live (\"this compress stuff is slowing down the dev server like 20 seconds\").\n- **CLOSED 2026-08-13** by PR #129's pin bump — the taxed file no longer exists, and the filed symptom is unreachable\n- Measured on the merged path (buildSurfaceClient → pruneAssets → precompressAssets): cold 3.87s · no-op rebuild 0.96s · changed-entry rebuild 2.18s\n- dist/assets stable at 19 files over four consecutive builds\n\n- **Leg (a) prune — paid upstream** (kolu#2159 names this downstream case in its own body): the hashed dir is reduced to what this build produced before anything is compressed.\n- **Leg (b) skip — paid upstream**: an identically-hashed sibling is left alone; the skip keys on emitted bytes, not source mtime (a minified-away comment does not re-pay).\n- **Leg (c) dev-skip flag — overtaken by ruling**: kolu#2159 refused the precompress knob BY NAME (\"the switch would only ever select between 'the layer works' and 'the layer has nothing to serve'\"). Not implementable in olai; re-opening it is a NEW upstream ask against that ruling, not this node.\n- **Residual, intended**: a build whose emitted bytes changed pays ~1.2s compressing the changed files (slightly up per-file — .zst is a third encoding now — while the total collapsed). Bounded, not growing.\n","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"editor-op-parity","parent":"bug-edit","ord":"a0","title":"Web editor expresses less than MCP — a standing HACKING consistency violation","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:42-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 from the human's review of the Editor subtree.\n- HACKING.md says \"MCP and Web ops must be consistent; never deviate\" — they had deviated, so this counted as a bug: the web editor could express less than the MCP tools.\n\n**Work complete 2026-08-14**; PR #166 awaits the human's approval (its working terminal `b9cc35e3` is still alive; the reviewer terminals are idle).\n- \"see\" references and \"after\" dependencies can now be written from the web: created through the same search widget the `((` shortcut opens, removed with an ×, and refusals from the cycle check are shown word for word and locked in by tests.\n- The sidebar can create outlines, showing the operation's own validation message word for word — all of it through the single intent seam, three new arms on the union type and three in the resolver, with no side channels.\n- **Panel review** — grok found no blocking issues and two small ones, opencode likewise, independently landing on the same two; both confirmed the inconsistency is closed. Everything was addressed in commit 9cbb779:\n  - The \"one verb\" prose was actually three verbs in four places.\n  - Test pins added for both newly excluded cases.\n  - A wrong reference in SeeRefs.tsx corrected.\n  - Undo of an edge is now covered end to end for \"after\" and for × removal (the leftover question of append order is asserted as documented behaviour).\n  - The edge-search test step now waits on the query rather than on time.\n- Five deliberate breakages were made on the evidence page, each proving the test it claimed to prove goes red.\n- 9/9 required CI checks at 9cbb779, mergeable and clean; 539 end-to-end scenarios, 1519 unit tests.\n- Evidence: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/2581c0b9-099a-4b44-bb6a-060669ec9d70\n\n- **Children closed by this PR at merge:** parity-see, parity-after, parity-create-outline (their done stamps land with the merge).\n- **Deferred, and named on the PR:** the append-order leftover itself (a change to set_see, not to undo), per-row createEdgeEditing signals, and edgesOf's algorithmic coupling to the planner.","see":["undo","input-widgets","silent-errors"],"changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"parity-archive","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a0","title":"archive: MCP archive_node vs no web affordance at all","done":"2026-08-18T02:55:00-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED for the mouse in PR #124** (`menu-verbs`, squash 60631fe, 2026-08-12); this stamp is the bookkeeping that never landed.\n- The human's ruling that day was SUBTREE WITH A CONFIRM, and that is what the `•••` menu's `Move to Trash` sends\n- One `archive` edit through the same gate `archive_node` goes through, behind a question naming how many rows go\n- Counted over the SET rather than over the rows on screen (done-hiding drops finished branches from a reading, and a count that read the page would understate what the write moves)\n- **NO KEY, deliberately:** there is still no delete key in this app, and the roadmap's own \"key only if the human wants one\" was never answered yes\n- Three doors reach the op now, all asking the same catalog (`web/src/client/menu/verbs.ts`): the row's `•••`, the multi-selection bar, and the ⌘K palette on a zoomed node — the one node no pointer could put away, since a heading has no `•••`\n- A MIRROR ROW IS NOT OFFERED IT (see `parity-remove-mirror`)\n- What the write did is on the Trash: `parity-unarchive` gave the archive its way out, and #226 took what is put away off every other page\n- Pinned by `menu_verbs.feature` (offers, asks, the count, cancel, confirm) and `trash.feature`; the verbs and the confirm are unit-tested beside them\n- **Stamped 2026-08-18** by the `web-archive-unmirror` lane, dispatched on this node's own stale desc — it still read \"the web has NO archive verb, key, or menu entry\", false since 2026-08-12\n  - Committed the evidence sections `move-to-trash-from-the-menu` and `retire-a-placement` (`packages/tests/evidence.ts`) so the pair is photographed rather than only asserted\n","changed":"2026-08-19T19:13:49-04:00"}
{"id":"parity-date","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a1","title":"date: MCP set_date (set AND clear) vs no web date editing","done":"2026-08-13T00:38:39-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #133 (95c3a8b), squash-merged 2026-08-13. The web can now set and change a date on a row: a date picker, reachable from the date badge and from the ••• menu (Set date… / Change date…), sending the same `date` edit that PR #124's Clear already put on the wire.\n\n- **Dates stay text.** `input type=\"date\"` was chosen because it cannot invent a moment in time — the ten characters picked are the ten written, and the only interpretation is @olai/format's dayOf. Deliberately breaking this by using toISOString made the test fail, as it should.\n- **One spelling of \"clear\".** The picker absorbs #124's separate verb: emptying the box turns the button into Clear date, and both routes emit an edit built by ONE constructor. A test proves the two routes cannot diverge (this was a must-fix from review, and was verified by deliberately re-splitting them to see the test fail).\n- **The agenda is the consumer, and that is proven:** a past date on a todo moves it into Overdue live, with no reload — locked in by an end-to-end test (deliberately breaking isOverdue turned 9 cases red).\n- **Producing the evidence caught two bugs before review** (an empty message box under the menu; \"Clear date\" being offered on a row with no date) — both fixed and covered by tests. The author also caught its OWN first end-to-end check passing even when the code was deliberately broken (waiting for something to be absent always succeeds) and rewrote it to read the state once.\n- **Recorded rather than lost**, in the PR body: undated rows have no touch route (the ••• menu is pointer-only below the md breakpoint — the same gap the checkbox has), plus three things the `/simplify` pass deliberately skipped.\n- **CI:** 10/10 on Linux at c186fca. The running file of unhealthy CI machines is now three for three (a kill in mid-suite at about 4½ minutes, a truncated log, and committing.feature untouched by this PR — the kolu-ci-2 and ci-3 runners implicated, ci-1 green throughout; the table is in the PR comment).\n- **Evidence** (verified by the orchestrator): the picker from the badge, the picker from the menu, the before/after move into Overdue, and the absorbed clear — archived to the session scratchpad.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"parity-marks","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a2","title":"todo/doing marks: MCP set_todo + set_doing vs web toggles done only","done":"2026-08-12T22:52:22-04:00","desc":"Shipped as PR #132 (f5d130d), squash-merged 2026-08-13. The web can now write all three marks without inventing a second writer: ⌘⇧Enter sends an INTENT (`walk`), and the server reads the stored mark and emits exactly the ops request an agent would have sent.\n\n- **The cycle:** unmarked, then todo, then doing, then back to unmarked. Unmarked is a real stop (the field leaves the record). `done` is deliberately not in the cycle: walking through it would stamp a completion time, fire the nudge, and hide the row on the way to somewhere else — Ctrl+Enter remains done's own key.\n- **The refusal is shown, not absorbed:** on a finished row the walk asks for todo outright, and the ops layer's own sentence is drawn under the row, naming the way through (undo first — two operations, the second one the person's).\n- **A bug found and fixed on the way:** `inverseOf` applied the two-edit undo whenever any mark displaced another; the rule is only meant for writes that leave a node done. Undo of the menu's Clear-mark was already broken by this. A regression test now covers it.\n- **Review:** no must-fix findings; six lines of attack held up, and three deliberate breakages of the code each turned the test that claimed to cover them red. Nice-to-have findings taken: a tautological mirror-on-disk assertion dropped, and coverage of the cycle's inverses widened. One was declined — adding markup purely for a test, since the sentence is already the contract through its test id. Menu verbs and Archive already existed from PR #124; this PR is keyboard, refusal and inverse only.\n- **CI:** 10/10 on Linux at the resolved head. The running file of unhealthy CI machines gained hard data: the base commit fails identically on the kolu-ci-3 and ci-4 runners with none of this PR's changes, and is green on a free pool.\n- **Evidence** (verified by the orchestrator): all four states in one glance, the walk step by step, the refusal word for word, the two-step route through it, the menu verbs, and the keys written down in docs/editing.md — archived to the session scratchpad.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"parity-see","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a3","title":"see edges: MCP set_see add/remove vs web renders but cannot write","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:37-04:00","desc":"MCP adds and removes see references; the web RENDERS them (edges-ui, done) but has no affordance to create or remove one. Creation wants a node search (the same widget family as input-widgets' `((` mirror search); removal wants an x on the rendered reference or a ••• verb."}
{"id":"parity-after","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a4","title":"after edges: MCP set_after vs web shows blocked state but cannot change it","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:38-04:00","desc":"MCP adds and removes after dependencies (#117 completed the set); the web draws blockedness (edges-ui) but a person cannot declare or lift a dependency. Same widget family as parity-see; acyclicity refusals must surface verbatim per HACKING."}
{"id":"parity-remove-mirror","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a5","title":"mirror removal: MCP remove_mirror vs no web way to retire a placement","done":"2026-08-18T02:55:00-04:00","desc":"**SHIPPED for the mouse in PR #124** (`menu-verbs`, squash 60631fe, 2026-08-12); this stamp is the bookkeeping that never landed.\n- The `•••` menu on a MIRROR row offers `Remove this placement` and does not offer `Move to Trash` — the split this verb exists for\n- What the reader is looking at is a line standing for a node that lives somewhere else, and archiving from there would file a subtree out of sight on a click that reads as being about this line\n- The question is asked of the row's OWN RECORD rather than of what it managed to draw (`web/src/client/menu/verbs.ts`), so the two degenerate placements — a chain that died, one that closed a loop — are covered by construction rather than by a fence\n- **THE OP'S FENCE REACHES THE CLICK VERBATIM:** a placement something else still names is refused naming what to re-point first, exactly as `remove_mirror` refuses it\n- Pinned by `menu_verbs.feature` (\"Retiring a placement takes the line and leaves the node\", \"A placement something else still names is refused, naming what\")\n- The creation half is `input-widgets`' `((`, whose ⌘Z is an `unmirror`\n- The retire itself is the one write on this surface with no inverse, said by recording nothing rather than by a ⌘Z that would mint a different id in a slot it cannot name (`server/src/edit.ts`)\n- **Stamped 2026-08-18** by the `web-archive-unmirror` lane, dispatched on this node's own stale desc — \"the web has no affordance on a mirror row\", false since 2026-08-12\n  - Also documented the verb in docs/editing.md (it was the one menu WRITE that doc never named)\n  - Committed the `retire-a-placement` evidence section\n","changed":"2026-08-19T19:13:49-04:00"}
{"id":"parity-create-outline","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a6","title":"create outline: MCP create_outline vs no web new-file affordance","done":"2026-08-14T12:33:38-04:00","desc":"MCP mints a new .jsonl outline (optionally seeded with a tree); the web sidebar lists files but cannot create one. A \"new outline\" affordance in the sidebar; naming/path validation is the op's, surfaced verbatim."}
{"id":"parity-unarchive","parent":"editor-op-parity","ord":"a7","title":"unarchive: missing on BOTH faces — the trash cannot be taken out of","done":"2026-08-13T19:05:02-04:00","desc":"The equal absence the 2026-08-12 review exposed: UNARCHIVE existed on NO face. **Shipped as PR #147 (squash 7457d9ab), 2026-08-13**, under the human's shape ruling.\n- The archive got a real web view named **Trash** (file and MCP vocabulary unchanged): sidebar entry, read-only rows, \"Put back\" per row\n- The ops verb born once, exposed on both faces\n- ⌘⇧Z genuinely brings a row back out (pinned, including a retitled-parent scenario added when the review's own suggestion stayed green under sabotage)\n- **The review's MUST-FIX** (Put back on a minted ancestor husk minted live duplicates — proven by planner runs) fixed red-first at the ops layer: the notASignpost fence (bare + a live twin at the landing) refuses on both faces identically\n- Put back deliberately NOT hidden client-side, to avoid recreating the faces split\n- Archive/remove-mirror menu verbs were #124's (verified, not rebuilt; archive relabeled \"Move to Trash\")\n- **Escalated to the human, still open:** does the set freeze archived work, or stay editable on both faces (today: editable, symmetric — recorded in editing-web.md's Open)\n","changed":"2026-08-19T19:13:49-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-restore-wrong","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a0","title":"Restart restores the wrong chat: newest-in-directory is an identity guess","done":"2026-08-13T23:12:05-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-14 on the human's report: after olai restarts, the panel restored some OTHER session.\n- Boot adopted the newest-by-updatedAt session in the served directory, an identity guess anything fresher could steal.\n- **MERGED 2026-08-13** (night slate, SIMPLE — autonomous merge per the human's overnight authorization): PR #156, squashed at master.\n  - The panel's session id is now olai's own note (chat `memory.ts`, under `$XDG_STATE_HOME`): written when the panel enters a conversation, read back at boot.\n  - Newest-in-directory demoted to first-boot/gone fallback.\n  - Picker rows carry honest local timestamps.\n  - CodeQL's polynomial-redos finding on the trailing-slash regex fixed by counting slashes.\n- **Evidence:** e2e scenario fails on the old `stored[0]` behavior, 50/50 scenarios pass with the fix.\n- **Grok review:** 1 blocking + 2 nits (all doc staleness), addressed at 935d5705; 9/9 required Linux checks green.\n- The superseded-by-hint dead end moved to its own node under Bugs (#upstream #human).","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"superseded-hint-upstream","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a1","title":"Session list can't show \"superseded by\" — the adapter gives no data #upstream #human","todo":true,"desc":"- The adapter's listSessions returns only {sessionId, cwd, title, updatedAt}, and ACP has no message count — so the planned superseded hint and per-row counts have nothing to read.\n- Any fix is upstream (adapter `_meta`, or ACP SessionInfo). Needs the human's go-ahead before filing on another repo.","changed":"2026-08-24T16:02:09-04:00","custom":{"route-update":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: the cross-repo route this node waited on is now ESTABLISHED practice — an upstream issue plus a vendored patch under acp/patches/ (the #365/#366 shape; claude-agent-acp#1038/#1039 filed this weekend). Also: SessionInfo now carries `agent` (packages/surface/src/chat.ts:924, from #343) — only the count/superseded data is still missing from the adapter's session/list. The #human gate here is only WHETHER to want the feature, no longer HOW to file upstream"}}
{"id":"undo-feature-flaky","parent":"bug-tests","ord":"a0","title":"undo.feature races a write against the assertion after it #techdebt","done":"2026-08-14T09:00:48-04:00","desc":"The e2e flake was one mistake in six places: a write goes out through a DRAFT, the draft is the only receipt this tab gives, and no key step waited for it.\n- **MERGED 2026-08-14** (PR #164, the human approved, squashed at 96b1dfb).\n  - \"I press\" / \"I type\" / \"I select all and type\" / \"I click away\" now wait for the caret to be in the line before aiming, and to leave the PLACE (`Row.key`: chain + seat) after — or for the page to say why not.\n  - \"I press … without waiting\" remains for the two scenarios that mean the race.\n  - Two disk assertions fixed: waiting readers go through `world.servedNodesSoFar` (ENOENT and nothing else — a non-JSON line still faults).\n  - The holding-negative \"holds no node titled\" split from its waiting twin (five holding uses remain, listed on the PR).\n- **Proof:** 5/5 undo runs and 3/3 five-feature runs failed under load before; 12/12 green after, 12/12 again post-`/simplify`, 3/3 after the review round.\n  - No timeout raised, no retry added, no claim loosened — red runs now name what they waited for (mutant-proven wording).\n- CI 9/9 required at 96b1dfb.\n- **Two product findings reported, not fixed**, each its own node under Bugs: new-line-blur-caret and client-quiescence (#human).\n- **MERGE-ORDER NOTE for the open PRs:** #159 and #162 each carry their own copies of the two disk fixes.\n  - Whichever lands after this conflicts textually in `editing_steps.ts` — keep THIS reader (`servedNodesSoFar`, ENOENT only) and one copy of the step.\n  - Their local `recordsIn` blanket-catch swallows malformed records and must not survive.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"client-quiescence","parent":"bug-tests","ord":"a1","title":"The client never says when it has finished handling a key #nightly","done":"2026-08-24T22:31:07-04:00","desc":"- When the client refuses a keypress it redraws nothing, so nobody — tests included — can tell a second refusal from the first. Under load this once corrupted a title that was being typed.\n- The client does acknowledge keys that move the caret, but keys that redraw in place (Ctrl+Enter and the mark walk) acknowledge nothing.\n- **The honest fix:** the client publishes when it has gone quiet — a counter raised when a key is queued and cleared when the queue drains. That covers every key at once, instead of one acknowledgement per key.\n- This is a change to the product made for the tests' sake, so it is the human's call.\n\n**Merge:** approved by the human at 20:33 through the question tool, once the grok review was folded in and CI was green.\n\n**Dispatched:** overnight.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-claude-opus","brief":"briefs/cq.md","dispatched":"2026-08-24T20:35:00-04:00","merge":"merge-auto","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/a1403535","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/379","ruled":"the human 2026-08-24 ~16:28 (question tool), all three ambiguities: (1) the counter is APPROVED — product code for observability, the wait-on-the-event doctrine applied to the client; (2) exposed as a data- attribute on the editor root (data-keys-settling counting down to \"0\"), the testids convention, no global and no test-only build; (3) scope is ALL keys — every key wait in the e2e suite migrates to the counter, one doctrine; the per-key receipt machinery stays for humans but stops being the test contract. Tagged #nightly — the human dispatches before sleep","worktree":".worktrees/client-quiescence"}}
{"id":"new-line-blur-caret","parent":"bug-edit","ord":"a1","title":"Clicking away from a brand-new line leaves the caret in the row it just made","done":"2026-08-15T16:47:25-04:00","desc":"FINDING from PR #164's era (2026-08-14): clicking away from a brand-new line left the caret in the row it just made.\n- **MERGED 2026-08-15** (pre-approved on the orchestrator's visual verification): PR #187, squashed at 4d031cb.\n- **Root cause** exactly the node's hypothesis: every caret keeps its address for life EXCEPT a brand-new line's.\n  - The write that lands it mints the row's id, so the blur guard read \"the reader moved\" about a reader who never did.\n- **Fix:** the landed row carries the slot it was typed at (`Editing.was` — one writer, one reader, always-a-row forwards nothing, sabotage-proven); blur asks `stillAt`.\n- Three red-first scenarios across all three anchors.\n  - The disclosed timing window (click inside `IDLE_COMMIT` or the idle timer masks the bug) proven asymmetric by review — a slow runner gets a loud timeout, never a silent pass.\n- **Reviews:** Grok + opencode, zero blocking, zero nits.\n- The orchestrator verified the before/after frames by eye: an open editor where the click should have landed, then nothing being typed.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"input-widgets-escape-flake","parent":"bug-edit","ord":"a2","title":"input_widgets.feature:209 flakes: Escape sometimes fails to close the editor draft #e2e","done":"2026-08-15T17:39:32-04:00","desc":"Spotted during PR #171's overnight test run (2026-08-14); grok worked out where it came from. It had broken at least five separate runs across that week.\n\n- **Hunted down 2026-08-15** (Grok authoring, terminal `06697db6`): the intermittent failure was real, and it was two bugs, not one.\n  1. Escape could bring back the draft it had just dismissed (reproduced in isolation, failing on run 3 of 200, with the state captured).\n  2. With only that fixed, a ⌘Z pressed too early still tripped it — failing at run 150.\n- Both are fixed in PR #190.\n- **Proof:** with both fixes, the scenario at input_widgets.feature:209 passed 300 out of 300 runs, its neighbouring scenarios 100 out of 100, and the full suite was clean; the unit test for `kept(null, held)` was proven to fail before the fix and pass after.\n- CI: 9 of 9 required checks green at d3e6a75.\n- **Stage:** in review — opencode is the sole reviewer, by the human's instruction (terminal `91e57e62`), briefed to work out both mechanisms again from the captured states and to repeat the repetition proof at its own timing.\n- Handed to srid; the merge is his decision.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"watcher-postboot-blind","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a7","title":"A directory created after boot is invisible to the watcher until the 60s backstop","done":"2026-08-15T21:36:05-04:00","desc":"Found 2026-08-14 by the watcher-fd-cost investigation, in what ships today (bun 1.3.13's watcher, Linux).\n- Create a file in a post-boot subdirectory → NO EVENT; edit it → NO EVENT.\n- Only `store.ts:157`'s 60s poll backstop keeps this from being a correctness bug.\n- Measured shape-by-shape; the method and transcript are in `docs/brainstorming/watcher-fd-cost.md`.\n- Fixed upstream by bun 1.3.14's watcher rewrite (the same bump that drops 1050 fds to 15).\n- This node closes with whichever bump path the human rules on bun-watcher-bump, and exists so the blind spot is not forgotten if that ruling waits.","see":["bun-watcher-bump"],"changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/196"}}
{"id":"tests-stray-debris","parent":"bug-tests","ord":"a2","title":"Stray probe files committed to packages/tests, one hardcoding a dead worktree path","done":"2026-08-15T17:47:07-04:00","desc":"Found by the sidebar-pierre-tree lane (2026-08-15).\n\n- **Found:** packages/tests/_h.ts and _probe.ts — agents' probe scripts accidentally committed, one hardcoding a dead worktree path.\n- **RESOLVED** the same day by the human's ruling: no CI check, just hygiene.\n  - Both files git-rm'd directly on master (718e312e).\n  - The human's own \"no junk files\" line committed to HACKING.md (aa23635e).\n- The check-that-would-catch-them idea lives on at stray-file-green-ci, unratified.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-model-stale","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a2","title":"The chat header's model goes stale after /model","done":"2026-08-15T11:16:32-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 by the human, with a screenshot: the chat header kept showing the old model after a `/model` command.\n\n- **Diagnosed as systemic:** the values in the model picker are the adapter's short aliases, while the id the running session reports at startup is the full canonical one.\n- Because of that mismatch, the label lookup missed all five rows, so the \"model is stale\" warning fired on every change that had ever been made, and the header showed either a raw id or a stale one.\n\n**Now** (2026-08-15):\n- Kolu terminal `485cd9bb`, branch `chat-model-stale`, PR #176 at head 46db630.\n- **Review round done:**\n  - Grok: one blocking finding — the code could construct a false label, showing \"Opus (1M context)\" on a 200k-context session — plus smaller notes.\n  - opencode: two smaller notes.\n  - **All addressed:**\n    - The guess that borrowed a label from a near-matching entry is gone: an undated live id resolves to a label, a dated pin is shown as a raw id, and the false \"Opus\" label is refused outright. One scenario drives all three paths. The deliberate cost: a common alias now shows as a raw id.\n    - The `if (running)` guard was deleted on its own merits — a refusal falls back to re-picking via config_option_update, which returns to whichever change moved last winning; a reconfiguration scenario pins that.\n    - A wrong video caption corrected.\n    - docs/chat.md now documents the refusal.\n  - **Deferred, argued on the record:** no test for the case where the first live id disagrees at boot — it would need a per-scenario spawn switch for a single assertion, and there is no baseline branch left to regress against.\n- **Stage:** CI running at 46db630 under the author's watch, then handed to the human. The second PR (chat-token-usage) is only cut after he rules on this one.","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"commit-op-staged-rename","parent":"bug-git","ord":"a3","title":"PR: pending and commit learn the index — a staged rename stops reading as a deletion","done":"2026-08-15T14:30:39-04:00","desc":"One hole, two faces, two live reproductions (2026-08-15): the pending/commit machinery reads the working tree against HEAD and never reads the INDEX, so hand-staged work — renames above all — is invisible or misclassified.\n\n- **MCP face** (orchestrator, fixing the docs/docs mint): with `git mv old new` staged, the commit op failed \"pathspec old did not match any files\" instead of committing the rename or refusing in its own words.\n- **WEB face** (the human's vault, post-.olai migration): `git mv`'d Archive.jsonl→Archive.olai and Tasks.jsonl→Tasks.olai staged.\n  - Commit panel showed \"Archive.jsonl deleted\" / \"Tasks.jsonl deleted\" — renames unread, the new .olai files invisible.\n  - Committing died with `fatal: pathspec '/home/srid/Vault/Archive.jsonl' did not match any files` — git's words surfaced raw because the sweep offered a path that exists only in HEAD.\n- **THE HUMAN'S CONSTRAINT**, binding on whoever takes this: REPRODUCE IN A TEST FIRST — a failing test holding a hand-staged rename against both the pending view and the commit path, before any fix lands.\n  - The fix reads the index (staged state) as part of pending, draws a rename as a rename, and never hands git a HEAD-only path.\n- **Related but separate:** create_outline minted docs/docs silently when handed the served root's own name — a refusal wanting a sentence (kept here so the two path-shaped stumbles travel together).","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"external-links-newtab","parent":"bug-nav","ord":"a2","title":"An external link opens in a new tab","done":"2026-08-15T12:31:56-04:00","desc":"Filed and fixed 2026-08-15 the same afternoon (the ledger was frozen at filing time — this node is the after-the-fact record, the freeze's one debt).\n\n- **Bug:** an external link rendered in olai navigated the app tab away.\n- **MERGED** as PR #180 (squash, review WAIVED by the human, Grok 4.6 solo author — its first authoring lane here).\n  - Cross-origin http/https anchors gain target=_blank rel=noopener noreferrer at render time.\n  - /doc/ links, node navigation and anchors untouched, pinned in render.test.ts's own style.\n  - CI 9/9 required at fbf49eee.\n- **Grok-TUI quirk** recorded for future dispatches: a paste during boot needs a second Enter once the footer says send-now.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"chat-diff-wrap","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a3","title":"PR: the chat's diffs wrap — no horizontal scrollbar in the panel","done":"2026-08-15T15:32:13-04:00","desc":"Filed 2026-08-15 by the human with a screenshot: an agent's file-edit diff in the ACP chat clipped long lines behind a horizontal scrollbar.\n\n- **MERGED** the same day (pre-approved on verified evidence): PR #185, squashed at 61f22f0.\n  - Content wraps inside the box (grid + the min-w-0 chain on Panel/Entry/ToolFrame/Transcript/DropTarget, overflow-x-hidden on the transcript, both halves credited).\n  - Gutter and +/- keep their column across wrapping add, remove and same rows, pinned with 700-character UNBROKEN tokens of each kind.\n- Grok authored; opencode sole reviewer per the human (nothing blocking, 3 nits fixed); CI 9/9 at head.\n- **Evidence:** the orchestrator verified the before/after by eye on laptop and phone before firing — the before shows the token escaping the box, the after wraps mid-token with the gutter intact.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"suite-load-flakes","parent":"bug-tests","ord":"a3","title":"Under load, the e2e suite drops 1–3 unrelated scenarios — never the same twice #e2e","done":"2026-08-15T23:47:22-04:00","desc":"Named independently by three lanes and two reviewers on 2026-08-15 (props, one-Site, caret, html-preview runs all saw it).\n\n- A full or tag-filtered suite on a box hosting other worktrees' servers intermittently fails 1–3 UNRELATED scenarios: inbox capture, a date pill, server restart, palette, edge editing, ⌘Z pair.\n- 15s timeouts, always green in isolation and on re-run, never twice on the same scenario.\n- Not any PR's diff — parallel workers vs machine load.\n- Distinct from the FIXED Escape flake (#190, a real bug).\n- **Hunt:** starts with worker-count vs load instrumentation; the fix may be as dull as backoff on the 15s step timeout under load — but measured, per the house law.","changed":"2026-08-24T16:02:00-04:00","custom":{"postscript":"2026-08-24 roadmap review: #199 bounded the class but did not end it — the identical shape recurred 2026-08-22–24 as the Inbox's e2e-localhost-load-flakes and took the #359–#364 campaign (esp. #363's six re-waits + two product landing races) to actually close","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/199"}}
{"id":"chat-speaker-discern","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a4","title":"PR: the human's chat messages are hard to tell from the agent's","done":"2026-08-15T18:38:28-04:00","desc":"Reported by the human 2026-08-15 with a screenshot (kept at /tmp/olai-dispatch/chat-discern-before.png).\n\n- In the web chat panel, the human's own messages (\"scrim?\", \"I confirm Esc does dismiss…\") render nearly identically to the agent's.\n- A faint box is the only cue, and it does not read as a speaker change.\n- **Fix:** visual differentiation of the human's messages in the chat transcript.\n- Dispatched to grok as AUTHOR at the human's explicit instruction, with no reviewer lane — a deliberate exception to agents.olai's reviewer-only contract for grok.\n- The PR must include before/after visual evidence (screenshots), per the human's dispatch order.","created":"2026-08-15T18:00:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-grok","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/191"}}
{"id":"client-draw-crash","parent":"bug-outline","ord":"a1","title":"The web page crashes while drawing: \"Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'remove')\"","done":"2026-08-16T11:07:05-04:00","desc":"Seen 2026-08-16, mid-chat, on the production build (myolai.rooster-blues.ts.net). The whole page dies to the \"This page broke\" screen.\n\n- **Window:** the human says it started in the last ~24 hours of PRs — #192 through #200.\n  - Of those, #192 hits-carry-custom, #195 search-props-ui and #197 html-frame-height touched client drawing; #200 was tests-only.\n- The throw is inside the UI framework's own list-reconciliation code, not olai's (frames `qf`/`ft`/`rn` in the minified bundle; `dev.js` in dev).\n- \"reading 'remove'\" during reconciliation usually means the framework tried to take a DOM node out of a list it no longer holds — the classic causes are two rows sharing a key, or a keyed list being mutated while it draws.\n- **Stack** (prod bundle main-hesa85kj.js):\n\n```\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'remove')\n    at qf (main-hesa85kj.js:1:22637)\n    at ft (main-hesa85kj.js:2:3293)\n    at Object.fn (main-hesa85kj.js:2:3019)\n    at rn (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7545)\n    at y (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7326)\n    at U (main-hesa85kj.js:1:1596)\n    at ft (main-hesa85kj.js:2:3011)\n    at Object.fn (main-hesa85kj.js:1:25143)\n    at rn (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7545)\n    at y (main-hesa85kj.js:1:7326)\n```\n\n- Not yet reproduced on demand.\n- **Next:** a fix PR should first find a reproduction (what was on screen: the chat page, while messages streamed), then bisect the #192–#200 window if the reproduction doesn't point straight at the cause.","created":"2026-08-16T09:33:17-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/202"}}
{"id":"html-preview-relative-links","parent":"bug-docs","ord":"a0","title":"HTML preview: clicking a relative link to another .html doc does not navigate there #deferral","done":"2026-08-16T17:55:15-04:00","desc":"RULED by the human at #206's gate (2026-08-16), with the example that settles it: folder A holds foo.html and bar.html; foo links to bar; clicking that link must open **bar.html in Olai**.\n\n- The app navigates to bar's own page (URL, sidebar selection, everything) — the same place clicking bar.html in the sidebar lands.\n- What #206 shipped is NOT this: the click swaps the bytes inside the preview frame (the sibling renders sealed, in-frame) while Olai's own page still says foo.html.\n- That in-frame behavior was #206's honest scope; this node is the remaining distance.\n- **Shape of the fix:** the frame's navigation (or a click intercept) tells the host page which vault file it moved to, and the host navigates the app to that document.\n  - Likely a postMessage from the injected prefix script, or intercepting same-vault link clicks before the frame navigates.\n  - Must stay inside the seal's rules: only same-vault .html targets get this treatment; everything else keeps #206's behavior.\n- Needs the e2e scenario the original filing asked for: click a relative link, assert Olai's own page is now bar.html's.\n- **Original filing, for history:** follow-up to #201, whose base made relative links 404 under /media/ — #206 then made them render in-frame; ruled insufficient.","created":"2026-08-16T09:35:51-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"from":"201","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/209"}}
{"id":"noderefs-duplicate-target-crash","parent":"bug-tasks","ord":"a2","title":"NodeRefs: a repeated see/after target crashes the page the same way #202 fixed","done":"2026-08-16T12:05:05-04:00","desc":"Found by #202's author while hunting client-draw-crash, deliberately left out of that PR.\n\n- NodeRefs.tsx draws see/after links with `<Key each={props.refs} by=\"id\">` — but an id names the *target*, not the row.\n- A hand-edited .olai repeating a target (`\"see\":[\"x\",\"x\",\"x\"]`), opened at /n/&lt;node&gt;, dies with the identical reconciler stack on the next store frame.\n- Verified against a real server.\n- **RULED** by the human, 2026-08-16: draw ONE — deduplicate at the read.\n  - Matches the write layer's set semantics (a re-add is a silent no-op in plan.ts): a repeated target means one link.\n  - Fix: a dedupe where the refs are read, keeping `by=\"id\"` honest.\n  - Plus its own scenario: a hand-edited duplicate target draws one pill and the page survives.\n- Reproduction and both options are written on PR #202.","created":"2026-08-16T10:21:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/203"}}
{"id":"chat-model-reverts-on-restart","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a5","title":"Chat: /model choice does not survive an olai restart — the session comes back on sonnet","done":"2026-08-16T22:52:24-04:00","desc":"Reported 2026-08-16: switch the chat to fable with /model, restart olai (new deploy), and the restored chat runs sonnet again.\n\n**ROOT CAUSE — CONFIRMED 2026-08-16.**\n- The deploy container's `~/.claude/settings.json` holds `\"model\": \"sonnet\"` (the human read it out).\n- The pinned adapter (0.66.0, `dist/acp-agent.js` ~5470-5530) picks the model on session/load by priority: (1) `ANTHROPIC_MODEL` env, (2) settings.model, (3) the resumed transcript's live model, (4) default.\n- For a RESUMED session it deliberately RE-ASSERTS an env/settings pin with `query.setModel` (\"a resumed session lands on the transcript's model regardless of env/settings, so the override must be re-asserted\").\n- `/model` is handled inside the wrapped CLI, so the change lives only in the transcript — which the pin then clobbers on every restart.\n\n**IMMEDIATE REMEDY (no code):** delete the `\"model\"` line from the container's `~/.claude/settings.json`.\n- `/model` choices then survive restarts via the transcript, and fresh sessions start on the adapter's default.\n\n**DURABLE FIX (if a settings pin should coexist with /model):**\n- olai remembers the session's chosen model itself and re-applies it after session/load through the model config option.\n- Or upstream: argue the transcript should outrank settings on resume.\n- **Floor either way:** `/model`'s change only becomes visible to olai one turn late (the next turn's init message), because nothing else on the wire carries it.","created":"2026-08-16T11:18:38-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/212"}}
{"id":"chat-subagent-live-face","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a6","title":"Chat: a running subagent has no live face — lanes only fill as tool frames arrive","done":"2026-08-17T01:11:57-04:00","desc":"Reported 2026-08-16 with a screenshot: during a fan-out, the panel shows the Agent call as a plain pending dot plus a lane of already-arrived tool rows — nothing says a subagent is running RIGHT NOW.\n\n**It IS possible in ACP:**\n- The pinned adapter streams a subagent's tool calls live, stamped with `parentToolUseId` in `_meta.claudeCode` (exactly what #193's lanes draw), with pending/completed status riding each frame.\n- The e2e suite proves the lane draws them (`the_agent.feature` 82-100).\n\n**What's actually missing**, in the client (`chat/src/agent.ts` ~420-470 and the lane drawing):\n- The Agent frame itself gets no live treatment — a running Task shows the same \"·\" as anything pending: no spinner, no \"running · N calls so far\", no elapsed time.\n- A subagent's TEXT and THINKING are never drawn: `agent_message_chunk` is emitted as \"said\" with the parent meta ignored, so between tool calls a working subagent produces nothing visible.\n  - If the adapter forwards its narration, it would land in the MAIN agent's voice — check whether that interleaving is reachable; that would be its own defect.\n- A subagent spawned but with no first tool call yet has no lane at all — nothing to hang the lane on until a tool frame arrives.\n\n**Fix shape:**\n- Give the Agent frame a running state (derived from its own tool_call status plus the presence of parent-attributed frames since).\n- Decide whether a subagent's narration deserves a collapsed line in its lane (Claude Code's own UI shows \"N tool uses · last: ...\" — a compact live summary, not the full stream).","created":"2026-08-16T11:18:52-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/214"}}
{"id":"html-preview-scripts-dead","parent":"bug-docs","ord":"a1","title":"HTML preview: the page's own JavaScript does not run — ruled unacceptable; adopt kolu's iframe shape","done":"2026-08-16T13:58:26-04:00","desc":"**Ruled 2026-08-16 by the human:** a previewed .html whose scripts are dead is unacceptable.\n- Today this is by design — the seal (#189, hardened in #197/#201) inlines the document via `srcdoc` with a CSP whose `script-src` allows exactly one hash (the seal's own measure script), so the file's scripts are blocked on purpose.\n- **Overruled:** #189's \"not negotiable\" line — both #197 reviewers had already asked for it to be amended.\n\n**How kolu does it** (the reference, read 2026-08-16): `kolu/packages/solid-fileview/src/renderers/iframe.tsx`.\n- Renders an iframe with `src` pointing at a file-serving route and `sandbox=\"allow-scripts\"` (WITHOUT `allow-same-origin`) — the page's JS runs in an opaque origin: cannot read host cookies/localStorage, CORS-gated fetch from inside is blocked.\n- The route (`kolu/packages/server/src/iframePreviewRoute.ts` + `packages/serve-dir`) serves raw bytes with content-type, guarded per-segment against traversal on the UN-normalized request target.\n- A postMessage bridge is available via ref for host↔frame channels.\n- The sandbox is the boundary; CSP hash-pinning is not part of it.\n\n**The olai shape of that:**\n- Serve the .html body by URL (extend the `/media` route family or a sibling `/preview` route, traversal-guarded like #201's mediaTarget but not picture-only).\n- Switch the frame from `srcdoc` to `src`, keep `sandbox=\"allow-scripts\"` alone.\n\n**Open design points for the PR to argue:**\n- **Privacy promise** (\"a saved page cannot tell anybody else's server what you are reading\"): an opaque origin still loads remote no-cors embeds (img/script src).\n  - If the promise is to survive, the route can send a Content-Security-Policy HEADER permitting inline/own-origin scripts but no remote hosts — JS works, nothing leaves.\n  - If the human would rather drop the promise, that's a deferral to rule.\n- **Measure script** (two-readings height, #197/#201): srcdoc injection dies with srcdoc. Options: inject into the served bytes server-side, a postMessage bridge like kolu's, or read the frame's height another way.\n- **Relative links:** with a real URL base, a relative link navigates INSIDE the frame to the sibling file — this may fix `html-preview-relative-links` for free; say so with a scenario if it does.\n- **Docs:** format.md's seal story and #189's \"not negotiable\" sentence must be rewritten to the new rule.\n\n**Security review non-negotiable on the PR**, as with #197/#201.","created":"2026-08-16T11:22:02-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/206"}}
{"id":"just-e2e-node-leak","parent":"bug-tests","ord":"a4","title":"just e2e breaks before running a scenario: a system node leaks into the nix e2e shell","done":"2026-08-17T01:51:47-04:00","desc":"Found by #206's author, 2026-08-16, and reproduced on a clean checkout of master — not that PR's doing.\n- `just e2e` fails before a single scenario runs: a system `node` leaks into `nix develop .#e2e` and cannot type-strip @kolu/surface's TypeScript sources under node_modules.\n- **Workaround the author used:** run the suite through `bun ./node_modules/@cucumber/cucumber/bin/cucumber.js` directly.\n- CI's e2e leg is unaffected (green).\n- **Fix direction:** the e2e dev shell should pin/front its own node (or run cucumber under bun) so PATH's node never wins.","created":"2026-08-16T12:15:59-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/216"}}
{"id":"done-over-open-work","parent":"bug-tasks","ord":"a1","title":"A done parent must not be able to hide unfinished tasks — gate both doors","done":"2026-08-16T15:26:01-04:00","desc":"Ruled by the human 2026-08-16 after the model-indices slices vanished from the roadmap view.\n\n**Symptom:** the Infrastructure root stored `done`, and done-hiding — correctly, per its own rule that a parent's mark is a claim about the whole branch — hid everything under it, including the four freshly-filed model-indices slices.\n\n**Root cause** (traced through git):\n- Nobody marked it over open children.\n- PR #90 (\"Status is stored, never derived\", 2026-08-11 morning) ended derived parent status and, as its data migration, STAMPED the then-derived rollup into storage — that morning every child under Infrastructure happened to be done, so the derived \"done\" froze onto the root as a stored fact (`\"done\":\"2026-08-11\"`; f69d26d0 reformatted it to an instant the same afternoon).\n- Then five days of new open children accumulated under a root that silently carried done.\n- The mark was true when written and went stale by design: a stored fact over a growing branch, with no constraint at either write.\n- **Sweep of the other roots:** `editor` and `website` also store done but currently hide nothing; `infra`'s mark was cleared in a40583f3.\n\n**The ruling:** this must be a preventable constraint, not a nudge — the history shows one gate is not enough, since the state is reachable through two doors:\n- **set_done over open work**: marking a node done while its SUBTREE holds unfinished tasks (todo/doing — an unmarked bullet is not a task, per #90's own creed, and must not block) is REFUSED, naming the unfinished tasks — the same refusal shape set_doing already has for `after`.\n- **Open work arriving under a done ancestor** — the door this incident actually walked through: add_node with a todo/doing mark, set_todo/set_doing, or a move that lands an unfinished task under a done ancestor. Choose and argue: refuse it, or auto-undo the ancestor's done (louder and kinder to capture flows), but never let it land silently.\n- Web and MCP share the planner, so each gate is written once.\n- Archived subtrees stay exempt as everywhere else.\n- The nudge that set_done currently returns becomes the refusal's message body.","created":"2026-08-16T13:09:12-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/207"}}
{"id":"agenda-shows-trashed","parent":"bug-dates","ord":"a0","title":"Agenda shows trashed nodes — archived dated work keeps its place on a page about what is owed","done":"2026-08-17T15:18:51-04:00","desc":"**Reported by the human 2026-08-17; RULED same day:** archived (trashed) nodes appear ONLY on the trash page — stripped from the agenda, day pages, and every other page.\n- This reverses the three-archived-doors doctrine #221 documented (trash, day, agenda): trash becomes the sole door.\n- **The fix must sweep:** agendaOf's keep and the \"archive keeps its place\" test (inverted, renamed); day-page collection; #221's Scope.archived (collapses to the trash page alone — the filter exception simplifies); docs/search.md and format README prose.\n- `is:archived` in search remains the explicit way to reach archived nodes from anywhere.\n- Dispatched to Opus.","created":"2026-08-17T08:51:48-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/226"}}
{"id":"menu-said-overlay","parent":"bug-nav","ord":"a3","title":"The menu's refusal line is painted over by sticky headings — the one overlay #233 left behind #deferral","done":"2026-08-19T20:47:22-04:00","desc":"The findings the human waived when he ruled that PR #233 could merge without a further review round (the claude reviewer's comment 5322681125, reproduced on the branch).\n\n- **(1)** menu/MenuSaid.tsx — the line that shows the ops layer's refusals — is still positioned inside the row at the row's own stacking level, so a sticky section heading paints over it. The README's stacking table still lists it as belonging to the row level, which contradicts the new doctrine in overlay.ts.\n- **(2)** The completions half of #233 fixed a second real bug with NO test (it fails on master and passes there), and it measures positions by hand where `@kobalte/core/popper` — already a dependency, already anchoring the menu — would give flipping, shifting and automatic repositioning for free.\n- **Smaller:** a `host.parentElement` lookup fails silently; there is no sweep in claims.test.ts for the new layering rule.\n\n**PR:** all three points closed — the refusal line moved onto overlay.ts, the popper library adopted, and the claims sweep added.","created":"2026-08-18T10:07:11-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"from":"233","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/a6bd9970","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/268"}}
{"id":"chat-links-reload","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a7","title":"Chat: a link in the agent's answer full-reloads the app — the transcript answers node chips, not anchors","done":"2026-08-20T11:29:34-04:00","desc":"FOUND by the human after `vault-in-browser` landed (2026-08-20): click a `.md` link in an ACP chat answer and the page reloads from scratch; the same file clicked in the sidebar does not. An audit of every link surface in `packages/web/src/client` found this is the ONE instance — the only internal anchors drawn outside a pane and outside a `<Link>`.\n\n**Why.** The app has exactly two ways an internal link stays in-app: it is a `<Link>` (`router.tsx`), or it is a plain `<a>` inside a pane's `<main>`, whose click bubbles to the pane's delegated listener. The chat panel is mounted BESIDE the panes, and its transcript's only click listener asked one question — `nodeRefIn`, the backtick node-id chips — so every anchor the agent's rendered markdown carried fell through to the browser's default.\n\n**DONE 2026-08-20 — #284 (Opus, merged 1c87295d1; grok do-not-object).** `router.tsx` gained `useFollow`, the one shared tail (`followedSplit` → `openRight`, else `followed` → `go`), called by PageView's listener and the transcript's after its chip check; `useHere` falls back to the focused pane, so a link pressed in the drawer lands where the palette and sidebar land. The fake ACP agent grew a `links` verb; two e2e scenarios click a `.md` link and a `/n/<id>` link (`the page has not reloaded`, the pane shows the target; Alt+click opens to the right). Refactor passes collapsed four wait/click/settle copies in test support onto `world.press(…, modifiers)`. docs/chat.md says it in one sentence.\n- Left for later, filed: `follow-one-unit` — fold `Link` and `Tree.tsx`'s own handler into `useFollow` (every navigation in the app, so not on a bug-fix PR).","see":["external-links-newtab","vault-in-browser"],"created":"2026-08-20T10:30:33-04:00","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"regressions","ord":"b00","title":"Regressions","desc":"Behavior that shipped worse than what it replaced, each node naming the offending PR. Ruled into existence 2026-08-15 by the human during the first live one.","changed":"2026-08-21T13:01:16-04:00"}
{"id":"stdio-retires","parent":"regressions","ord":"a0","title":"PR: the stdio MCP retires — agents dial the running server over HTTP","done":"2026-08-15T14:54:33-04:00","desc":"RATIFIED 2026-08-15 after the bridge regression's root-cause conversation: one brain, and the door to it is HTTP.\n\n- The `olai mcp` subcommand is DELETED; .mcp.json points at the running server's /mcp route (the user runs `just run`); the ACP chat keeps its self-server MCP unchanged.\n- **Rulings sealed at the door:**\n  - (a) LOOPBACK NEEDS NO TOKEN — requests from 127.0.0.1 skip auth on /mcp, the chat keeps its bearer tokens, same trust stdio had.\n  - (b) The #175 attach/unix-socket machinery goes WITH it — serveOverUnixSocket usage, the dial, OwnedSurfaceConnection wiring, dead code deleted not dormant (the kolu per-face-expose upstream work stays: the web face rides it).\n  - (c) Stable per-repo port so the .mcp.json URL holds — just run binds it.\n- Docs move with it: .mcp.json example, docs/running.md, the charter gains the precondition that unattended agent runs need the server up.\n- This PR obsoleted three nodes filed hours earlier (bridge-no-fallback, socket-litter, mcp-process-leak — all archived with their stories).\n- **ASSIGNED:** Grok, solo, review WAIVED by the human.\n- Fielded to the human at green — merging changes his own session config (.mcp.json → http) and his run habits, so the coordination note rides the fielding.","changed":"2026-08-19T19:01:42-04:00"}
{"id":"brain-lock","parent":"regressions","ord":"a1","title":"PR: the brain locks its directory — a second olai over the same vault is refused","done":"2026-08-15T16:23:56-04:00","desc":"RATIFIED 2026-08-15, the enforcement half of one-brain.\n\n- **MERGED** the same day (pre-approved on verified evidence): PR #186, packages/server/src/lock.ts.\n  - bun:ffi flock, zero dependencies — the lockfile-family libraries failed the human's own kill-9 requirement (their validity is file-existence plus a staleness window).\n  - Keyed on realpath, pid in the file as DIAGNOSIS only.\n- **Evidence:** the orchestrator verified the transcript by eye.\n  - Second olai refused, naming pid.\n  - Symlinked spelling refused identically.\n  - kill -9 left the lock file on disk and the next boot served anyway — validity is the kernel's.\n- **Review:** Grok + opencode, nothing blocking.\n  - The EINTR disagreement settled IN the docstring with both readings and a bounded retry.\n  - kimi's CodeQL HIGH fixed at the line (0o600).\n- The audit's prediction came true: e2e workers were four olai over one shared fixture vault — per-worker copies now, which also closed a stray-write-into-tracked-fixtures hole.\n- **OPEN FOR THE HUMAN**, reviewers split: LockUnavailable (cannot determine a holder) ships FAIL-CLOSED with no escape.\n  - kimi argued a narrow bypass for only that case (macOS is CI-unproven; a portability miss is a no-dial outage).\n  - grok argued a macOS miss should be a bugfix, not a knob.\n  - Both paths' tests already exist either way.\n- **Cosmetic residue:** the VaultInUse refusal prints inside a raw Effect stack trace rather than as one clean sentence.","changed":"2026-08-19T19:01:42-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-after-the-flip","parent":"regressions","ord":"a2","title":"PR #279: the client stops respecting fine-grained reactivity","done":"2026-08-20T18:14:30-04:00","desc":"After PR #279 the client stops respecting fine-grained reactivity: the sidebar folds and relights on every click, and there are nine other remount sites.\n\nREPORTED by the human, 2026-08-20: a click on a sidebar entry opens the page in the pane (good) but the whole sidebar re-renders and flickers — which says the flip disrespected Solid's fine-grained reactivity. The ask was every such violation, not just this one.\n\n**The audit is `brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md`** — read it in full before touching anything; it is written so an agent can finish this autonomously: §1 the symptom measured in a browser (16 of 178 sidebar elements destroyed navigating within one folder; 0 across top-level files; the pane remounts with a `Reading…` beat every time), §2 the two framework facts (a kolu stream blanks to `undefined` on every input change; kolu's `reconcile(next, {key:null})` without `merge` replaces every array element per frame — verified against Solid 1.9.14), §3 every finding with `file:line`, severity and a one-line fix, §4 what is already right and must not be touched, §5 the six PRs, §6 the reproduction and the probe that becomes the test.\n\n**The root of the reported symptom**, in one line: `App.tsx:118-212` derives the sidebar's active file, the calendar's open day, the palette's names and undo's file from the focused pane's READING — which blanks for a round trip on every navigation — instead of from the ROUTE, which already knows. Pre-flip it read the route.\n\nThe children are the PRs, in §5's order. PR 1 first (it is the symptom); the rest are independent. The law is `vault-in-browser.md` §6's and HACKING.md's: self-contained PRs, a test per fix, CI green through odu.","see":["vault-in-browser"],"created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T18:14:30-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-route-not-reading","parent":"reactivity-after-the-flip","ord":"a0","title":"PR 1: the chrome reads the route, and a navigation swaps the page instead of tearing it down","done":"2026-08-20T12:19:09-04:00","desc":"`brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md` §3.1 (findings 1.1–1.8) and §5 PR 1.\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — #285 (Opus, merged e52955f4f; grok do-not-object after re-running the probe itself).** `reading.tsx` holds the last answer across the next question, so `undefined` means only \"this pane was never answered\" and a navigation SWAPS the page instead of tearing it down; `App.tsx` reads `openFile` off `fileNamed(route)` and `openDay` through `requestFor` — the page consulted only for what an address cannot say; `PageView.tsx` gates the FilterBar on `narrowable(route())`; `filter/asking.ts` stops gating its question on the page having rows and asks at once for a query that arrived with an address.\n- Measured (the §6 probe, two client builds, one corpus): sidebar elements destroyed on a within-folder click 11 → 0, three folders deep 37 → 0, a second day of a non-current month 60 → 0 with 144 → 6 sidebar mutations and the month no longer flipping, `aria-current` moving in one observer batch, a `?q=` page drawing one row instead of six-then-one.\n- Tests: four scenarios in `the_chrome_holds_still.feature`, one in `undo.feature`, one line in `pin_to_sidebar.feature` — all six red against master's bundle first. The probe is `packages/tests/support/probe.ts` with reusable steps (`the sidebar did not remount`, `the folder \"…\" stayed open`, `the current mark moved in one frame`, `the month never changed from \"…\"`, `the node \"…\" was never drawn`) for PRs 2–5.\n- Named cost of route-only: a `/#id` that names nothing, and an outline that will not parse, now draw a filter box where they drew none.","created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-20T12:19:09-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-for-by-reference","parent":"reactivity-after-the-flip","ord":"a1","title":"PR 2: nine repeated lists redraw wholesale because they have no key","done":"2026-08-20T13:18:34-04:00","desc":"The findings in `brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md` §3.2 (items 2.1–2.10), scoped as PR 2 in that document's §5. Nine places drew lists coming off the wire without telling the framework how to identify a row, so every row was destroyed and rebuilt on each frame: breadcrumbs, referrers, edge chips, property chips, pins, palette hits, the @-mention rows, the header search box and the refusal lines.\n\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — PR #287** (Opus authoring, merged 5c59c7176; grok raised no objection and no findings). Each of the nine sites now takes the key the audit's table names: `<Key by=…>` where rows have their own identity and can move (breadcrumbs, referrers, edge chips, property chips, pins, palette rows), and `<Index>` where position is what matters (the @ menu, the header box, the refusal lines) — the same idiom Shortlist, Completions, NodeRefs and TrashPage already used. Item 2.10: the names table moved into `names.ts` and is compared by value (the arrangement served.tsx already used); `namesIn`, two duplicate memos and three copies of the refusal sentence were deleted (`refusals.ts`).\n- **Measured** over the same nine scenarios on two builds: elements destroyed per identical frame went from 4 of 6, 3 of 6, 3 of 4, 8 of 9, 16 of 19, 22 of 23, 5 of 6, 22 of 24 and 1 of 7 on master to zero everywhere. The filter bar's `role=\"alert\"` line is no longer re-announced, and the caret stays on the edge panel's × when a write happens on disk (on master it was sent to the page body).\n- The region probe joined PR 1's `support/probe.ts` (`markScreen` built on `markRegion`) instead of standing beside it as a second tool.\n- **Seen but not touched, and filed:** `names-table-once` (App.tsx and each pane both scan the focused pane's names, twice per frame) and `one-alert-row` (the palette and header box hand-roll the alert row that SaidLine already draws). Item 2.11 — Tree.tsx re-running its per-row binding — belongs to the upstream kolu item.","created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-equals-guards","parent":"reactivity-after-the-flip","ord":"a2","title":"PR 3: three missing equality checks and one stream that never resets","done":"2026-08-20T14:39:09-04:00","desc":"Three values recomputed when nothing changed, plus one stream left open: the HTML preview reloads when the OTHER pane navigates, the composer re-asks its question on every keystroke, and the move-to picker's stream never closes.\n- The findings are in `brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md` §3.3 (items 3.1–3.4 and 4.7), scoped as PR 3 in §5.\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — PR #286** (Opus authoring, merged 2326e53c4; grok objected, the objection was addressed, then no objection). Item 3.1: the preview now holds while the other pane navigates — the `/simplify` pass replaced the string key with a `useLanding()` helper in router.tsx, which the faces.tsx work in item 4.9 can reuse. On master the preview page was visited twice instead of once; here it is unchanged, proven with side-by-side videos of a running tally surviving. Item 3.2: `subjects` gained an equality check (`sameIds`) — the number of node lookups for a typed word with a chip armed fell from 23 to 0. Items 3.3 and 3.4: `standing` now resets when the message line expires, so the move stream closes (the leaked frame went from 1 to 0, proven by deliberately breaking the fix), and the request is memoised on the pair (record, destination), so re-subscriptions went from 2 to 0.\n- Grok's one objection: the `/simplify` pass had dropped the reset of the picker's destinations, so a second picker still showed the previous list. Restored at commit 427ac38 with a scenario written to fail first; the move-to picker suite passed 15 of 15.\n- **Disclosed, not caused by this PR:** two local full-suite intermittent failures (the agent transcript's autoscroll under load, and scroll restoration in zoom_and_navigate), both proven to pre-exist on the merge base.","created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-per-frame-tax","parent":"reactivity-after-the-flip","ord":"a3","title":"PR 4: work repeated every frame — a whole-vault search, two copies of the page, and six memos with no equality check","done":"2026-08-20T14:31:28-04:00","desc":"The per-frame tax: a whole-vault search per frame, two structuredClones of the page per frame for a counter, and six memos without equals.\n- `brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md` §3.3 (3.5–3.7), §3.4 (4.1–4.6, 4.8) and §5 PR 4.\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — #290 (Opus, merged 50a2f281a; grok do-not-object, its own clone-probe and wire-count runs).** The page counts its frames off the store (`frames.ts`, a declared stand-in for kolu 5.2): master cloned the whole page twice per frame (≈1,458–1,684 kB on a 2,000-row page) — now zero. Nine memos gained `equals`/`createSelector`/stopped re-deriving; `editing.tsx`'s triple flatten gone; OFF-LIST: `select/selection.ts` flattened the visible tree twice per frame on every page — found by the cleanup pass, fixed, declared.\n- §3.5's coalescer: reproduced (9 whole-vault search.matching per bulk gesture), built both ways, measured 9 → 8, DELETED — no honest client window helps; the `equals` on `matched` landed. The standing cost is `filter-ask-carries-revision` #deferral.\n- What it left the kolu-upstream node: frames.ts retires with 5.2; its walk's 5.1 tripwire is frames.browsertest.ts's third case; a reconnect re-asks one extra match until then.","created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-small-c","parent":"reactivity-after-the-flip","ord":"a4","title":"PR 5: five small stale-screen bugs","done":"2026-08-20T15:49:04-04:00","desc":"The five small stale-screen items: a landing that re-scrolls when the file is rewritten, indenting a line jumping the caret to the end, Enter picking the previous query's row, a `settling` flag that gets stuck, and a failure slot where the last message wins. They are items 4.9–4.13 in `brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md` §3.4, scoped as PR 5 in §5.\n\n- **DONE 2026-08-20 — PR #294** (Opus authoring, merged 3a344f8f4; grok raised no objection, having run the scenarios itself). Four were reproduced and fixed, each with a scenario proven to fail on master first:\n  - 4.9: a reader scrolled to 978px stays there.\n  - 4.10: typing produced \"pick the knobsed\" instead of \"picked the knobs\"; the caret rule is now a `rekeys(edit)` table sitting beside the existing `redraws(edit)` table.\n  - 4.12: no `see` reference is written to a node the query has already moved past; the gate lives in Shortlist.tsx and completing.tsx, and the `/simplify` pass moved it from the take to the key press, which stopped it swallowing a pointer click on a stale row.\n  - 4.13: covered by a browser test.\n- 4.11 was DROPPED: it did not reproduce, driven five different ways. The audit's §3.4 row now carries the argument (no write reachable from the editor leaves this page's reading identical while still owing a frame), and redraws.ts's header states the precise version after grok's note.\n- **The decision made:** a stale Enter is consumed and spends nothing, silently — dimming was rejected as more flicker in a campaign about flicker; docs/editing.md says this in the reader's own words; a pointer press is never gated.\n- **Deferrals filed:** `shortlist-row-freshness` and `router-owns-spent-landing` (#deferral). Disclosed as local-only: serve.test.ts binds port 7714 and fails when the production service is running.","created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00"}
{"id":"reactivity-kolu-upstream","parent":"reactivity-after-the-flip","ord":"a5","title":"Upstream (kolu): keyless reconcile and the frame tracker's clones","done":"2026-08-20T18:14:27-04:00","desc":"Two problems in the kolu framework: `reconcile` called without a key replaces every element of an array on every frame, and the frame tracker clones the whole page twice per frame.\n\nThe analysis is in `brainstorming/reactivity-after-the-flip.md` §2 Fact B and §3.5. The work happens in the kolu repository first, then olai bumps its npins pin.\n- `@kolu/surface/src/solid/writeValue.ts` calls `reconcile(next, {key:null})` with no `merge` option. It should let a stream or collection declare which field identifies its array elements (`key` for rows, `id` for names, breadcrumbs and references) and pass `merge: true` for arrays that genuinely have no key. This is the root of every site listed in §3.2 of the audit, and of the per-row binding in `Tree.tsx` that re-evaluates once per row per frame (audit item 2.11), which has no fix on the client side.\n- `createSubscription.ts` from line 306, in `createUpdatedTracker.noteFrame`: two `structuredClone` calls plus a deep `framesEqual` comparison on every frame, as soon as any `updated` handler exists. It needs a counting mode that does not clone.\n- Verified against Solid 1.9.14 with the replay in §6: on an identical frame, `rows[$TRACK]` and `rows[0].node.title` each notify once today; zero is the target.\n- After it lands: bump the pin here, and delete whatever audit items 2.10 and 3.6 had to work around on the olai side.\n\n**What PR #290 (PR 4) left for this item, 2026-08-20:** `packages/web/src/client/frames.ts` is a declared stand-in for the framework's counting mode, and its header says so — it counts frames off the store, and when the framework provides the counter, `createReading` will read it and both `frames.ts` and `frames.browsertest.ts` go away. It also depends on today's keyless merge: once a stream can declare an array key, the walk must descend into array elements, and the third case in `frames.browsertest.ts` is the tripwire for that, deliberately not fixed in advance. Counting off the store also drops the tracker's third clause (an identical snapshot after a reconnect is silent), so a reconnect re-asks one whole-vault match more than it needs to; the framework's counting mode gives all three clauses back. PR #287 also left audit item 2.11 and the equality check on `names.ts` to this item.\n\n**Upstream PR:** the olai half — bumping the pin — runs as the final lane of the campaign.","created":"2026-08-20T10:51:13-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/b6378c576","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/kolu/pull/2190"}}
{"id":"redos-acp-diffs","parent":"bug-chat","ord":"a9","title":"A regular expression that gets slow on hostile input runs on agent input in acp/diffs.ts #security","done":"2026-08-20T22:20:33-04:00","desc":"CodeQL code-scanning alert 15 (rule js/polynomial-redos, severity high). In `relativeTo`, the call `cwd.replace(/\\/+$/, \"\")` at packages/acp/src/diffs.ts line 84 strips trailing slashes from a path that arrives over the protocol from the agent library. On a string with many slashes that are NOT at the end, the matching work grows with the square of the length, so a long crafted path can stall the process.\n\nFix: trim in one linear pass. The same PR should also fix the twin trailing-slash trim in the chat package, which the doc comment already names.\n\nDispatched to Grok on 2026-08-20 shortly after 21:00; reviewer opencode; to be merged automatically on the human's word.\n\n**PR:** `relativeTo` now counts trailing slashes instead. Alert 15 should close on the next CodeQL analysis of the default branch — verify that it does.\n\n**Merge:** pre-approved — merge automatically once review findings are addressed and CI is green.","created":"2026-08-20T20:41:34-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-20 (code-scanning alert)","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/23b5b937","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/304"}}
{"id":"plain-estimate-nested-emphasis","parent":"bug-outline","ord":"a2","title":"Nested bold/italic in a title renders as escaped source #next","done":"2026-08-22T02:22:26-04:00","desc":"- A title like `a **b *c* d** e` — bold with italics inside it — is not rendered; it falls back to showing the escaped source, with a stray `*` left over. The fault is in the `plainTextEstimate` function in markdown/title.ts, which guesses how much plain text a title contains and gets nested emphasis wrong.\n- The bug pre-dates PR #316; it was found while probing that PR. Until it is fixed, #316's behaviour of refusing to highlight rather than highlighting wrongly is the honest one.\n\n**PR:** the fourteen-regex `plainTextEstimate` was deleted. The check that compares against the source now uses `mdast-util-to-string` over markdown's own parse, behind the same entry point the renderer uses. Nested emphasis now renders in every spelling, as do tags and links next to it and `Tom & Jerry` written with an HTML entity. The rule about when to refuse is unchanged. Three assumptions are flagged for the human: a picture in a title still falls back; the length check remains one-directional, as on master; and `mdast-util-to-string`'s defaults are taken as-is.","created":"2026-08-21T10:20:28-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"from":"#316 review (pre-existing, named so nobody mistakes it for that change)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/7a2a4f62","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/339"}}
{"id":"e2e-log-truncation-flake","parent":"bug-tests","ord":"a5","title":"e2e CI red is still un-diagnosed — the log class that hid it is FIXED upstream","done":"2026-08-24T16:01:55-04:00","desc":"- **The second of the two problems here — logs being cut off before they were delivered — is FIXED.** odu#89 (commit 3d0db227, 2026-08-21) moved the step that joins a job's status to its log out of teardown and into the point where the verdict is decided; this came after more than twelve sightings. A failing run's log now arrives with both its tail and its summary.\n- **The first problem remains OPEN:** the rare, genuine end-to-end failure. It failed on the first attempt three times on PR #327's branch and was never identified, because the old truncation destroyed every chance of diagnosis. There is nothing to do but watch: the NEXT failing end-to-end log will finally carry the name of the failing scenario — record it here when it appears.\n- **History:** it was seen on PR #296 (3 times), #309, #313, #317, #318, #321 (3 times), #322, #327 (3 times) and #328, across at least four machines. The forensic work that cracked it — a job reporting `settled: true` while the file was still being written 25 seconds later — is on odu#89.","created":"2026-08-21T11:39:22-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"from":"orchestrator pattern-match 2026-08-21; sightings: #296 (3×), #309, #313, #317, #318, #321 (3×), #322 (passing run), #328, #327 (3× consecutive, ACTIVELY BLOCKING diagnosis — the truncation is hiding a possibly-real failure exactly as the node predicted) — twelve-plus occurrences; the odu drain fix is now urgent and awaits the human's cross-repo ratification","resolved":"2026-08-24 roadmap review — the node's \"nothing to do but observe, capture the next named red\" is satisfied and exceeded: the upstream log fix (odu#89) let the reds be NAMED, and the named class was then FIXED by the #359–#364 flake campaign (six e2e scenarios re-waited + two product landing races in #363; serve/pending/reaper/lock unit flakes in #359/#360/#361/#364). The residue lives as the Inbox flaky-tests newborns (menu-actions-test-flaky, loglevel-test-stoppedwithin)","upstream-fix":"odu#89 merged 3d0db227"}}
{"id":"manifest-fold-skew","parent":"bug-server","ord":"a9","title":"The error page can show while file paths are already loaded #next","done":"2026-08-22T02:08:11-04:00","desc":"- A mismatch that pre-dates the change that found it (spotted in PR #317's review): `Directory.manifest` can still be null — which makes the app draw the error page — at a moment when the separate accumulated view of the directory has already been seeded. The result is that live file paths sit hidden behind an error page.\n- It lives in the window inside runtime.ts where the two are deliberately allowed to disagree for a moment. Small, but real.\n\n**PR:** `Directory.manifest` becomes `Directory.standing`, one of 'reading', 'never' or 'loaded', resolved from both the data cell AND the accumulated view in the single place that holds both. Both orders of the mismatch are handled. The wire format is untouched. The cross-package invariant is pinned by a server-side test, and the driver that provokes the mismatch is committed as packages/tests/skew.ts. Three assumptions are flagged for the human: known paths outrank a stale null; the rename is in scope; and there is no cucumber scenario for the window itself.","created":"2026-08-21T12:00:18-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:00:17-04:00","custom":{"from":"#317 review (grok's leak hunt; pre-existing, outlives that branch)","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/950d7625","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/338"}}
