{"id":"cq-implement","parent":"client-quiescence","ord":"a0","title":"Add the counter, move the whole test suite onto it, open the PR","done":"2026-08-24T22:02:30-04:00","desc":"Reported at 22:01 (terminal e2c7887c, 1 hour 23 minutes of work): PR #379 at commit d04ed544. It adds the `data-keys-settling` counter on the editor root, following all three of the human's rulings, with an honestly stated boundary defended in the PR body.\n\nThe whole test suite was migrated, guided by a table organised by shape (about 21 sites across nine kinds of wait). The places deliberately left alone are argued in the PR: presses that intentionally do not wait, `.fill()` (which dispatches no keydown), the pointer acknowledgements in caret.ts (which shrank from 366 to 162 lines), and the four drivers.\n\nLeak safety: 14 unit tests with deliberate delays injected — refusals release the counter, a queued step that throws releases without wedging, a double release still counts once, and five keys ending in a refusal end at zero and stay there.\n\n**The validation is in the numbers:** three full end-to-end runs. The baseline was 929 of 930 — the one drop was a key race at one of the seven migrated sites, and it did NOT drop on this branch, because the counter absorbed it. The branch head ran 930 of 930 scenarios and 8080 of 8080 steps. Its own single drop was PR #376's scroll-restore intermittent failure, which has since merged, so merging master picks up that fix.\n\nLocal checks: typecheck, 3660 unit tests plus 77 browser tests with none failing, and the full end-to-end suite. CI was correctly left to the closing step. No deferrals.\n\n**PR:** at commit d04ed544.","created":"2026-08-24T20:37:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:01:52-04:00","custom":{"pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/379","terminal":"e2c7887c"}}
{"id":"cq-review-grok","parent":"client-quiescence","ord":"a1","title":"Review by the grok agent","done":"2026-08-24T22:15:54-04:00","desc":"The grok agent reviewed at commit d04ed544 and asked for the findings to be folded in: no must-fix, two should-fix (finished 22:15 after 12 minutes 15 seconds, reviewing by reading). Comment: https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/379#issuecomment-5404167669\n\nVerified: every increment of the counter is structurally paired with a decrement; the leak test suite covers the shapes it names; the migration matches the by-shape table; and the hot path is one signal write plus a `setAttribute` inside a render effect, with no re-render of the whole app.\n\n- **Should fix 1 — the stated boundary is untrue.** quiescence.ts line 92 claims that a debounce timer's eventual write is held by the counter, but `held()` and `holding()` both key off the count being above zero, which is cleared when the key dispatch ends. So the idle commit and the shortlist's async run actually start as pointer writes. The test suite already waits on IDLE_COMMIT and `data-asked`, so it is only the SENTENCE that lies — fix the text.\n- **Should fix 2 — a window where the counter reports zero too early.** quiescence.ts line 279 arms a 250ms fallback timer alongside `requestAnimationFrame`, even on a visible tab; a load hitch longer than 250ms would report zero before the two paints happen. Either limit the fallback to hidden tabs or lengthen it honestly.","after":["cq-implement"],"created":"2026-08-24T20:37:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:01:52-04:00","custom":{"reviewer":"agent-grok","terminal":"61603005"}}
{"id":"cq-close","parent":"client-quiescence","ord":"a2","title":"Fix the review findings, run CI once, merge automatically if green","done":"2026-08-24T22:31:07-04:00","desc":"Closed at 22:32. Both review findings were fixed at commit e11a4094:\n1. The sentence describing the boundary now tells the truth — the idle commit and the shortlist's async run are pointer writes, and the suite waits on IDLE_COMMIT and `data-asked`.\n2. The 250ms fallback timer is GONE. The path that waits for painting is `requestAnimationFrame` and nothing else; hidden tabs are ASKED about instead (checking `visibilityState` when scheduling, plus a `visibilitychange` listener that is removed on every exit path), with the frame counting expressed over a named `Painting` value so the rule can be tested without a browser.\n\nTests went from 14 to 18, with the header naming the pair of dangers (staying high while idle, and reaching zero too early), including the assertion the old fallback timer could never have made: tasks that produce no frames do not move the count.\n\nMaster merged cleanly at dbabc9e4; local checks 3679 unit tests plus 77 browser tests, none failing; CI run once, green. MERGED as squash commit a1403535 on the human's approval through the question tool; the worktree was removed, the branch deleted and terminal e2c7887c retired.","after":["cq-review-grok"],"created":"2026-08-24T20:37:14-04:00","changed":"2026-08-27T19:01:52-04:00"}
{"id":"chip-blur-double-commit-2","ord":"b02","title":"A chip's Enter commits twice — the value box's blur fires after the commit unmounted it, drawing a spurious nothing-would-change note","done":"2026-08-25T21:18:00-04:00","desc":"Enter in a chip's value box commits; the box unmounts; its blur handler then fires a SECOND commit, which the no-change guard converts into a spurious \"already says … — nothing would change\" note under the run. Cosmetic but every chip edit draws it. Fix shape: the blur handler stands down once Enter has committed (the commit owns the close). Worth its own small lane; pi knows the editor from #373/#395.","created":"2026-08-25T12:27:19-04:00","changed":"2026-08-25T21:18:05-04:00","custom":{"agent":"agent-pi","brief":"briefs/cbd.md","dispatched":"2026-08-25T18:52:00-04:00","from":"#395's author, 2026-08-25 — reproduced on an untouched corpus with no declarations: pre-existing (#373's chip editor), unrelated to typing","merge":"merge-human","merge-commit":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/commit/64546fb07b6cdfc9fbacaae8bf0e93062c29676f","merged":"2026-08-25T21:16:00-04:00","pr-url":"https://github.com/juspay/olai/pull/401","worktree":".worktrees/chip-blur-standdown"}}
{"id":"last-good-banner-flood","parent":"bug-outline","ord":"a3","title":"One broken outline's validator rows flood every page's last-good banner","todo":true,"desc":"Sighted by the human 2026-08-25 (screenshot, `git.md` open): with `orchestrator/lanes.olai` failing typed-property validation (~135 rows — `agent` got \"claude-opus\", `repo` got \"olai\", two rows per lane node), the \"Showing the last good version\" banner at the top of EVERY page inlined the FULL enumeration of the broken file's rows. The selected page's own content was still there below, but the banner ran longer than a screen, so every page opened on a wall of another file's errors.\n\nThe banner's job is \"your disk no longer validates, last-good is served\" — one sentence per broken file plus a row COUNT, with the full rows on the broken file's own page (which already draws them). Unbounded inline enumeration on unrelated pages is the bug.\n\nProvenance: the typed gate's first live day — the #395 migration left the unversioned lanes.olai violating ~135 rows until the orchestrator migrated it, which made the flood easy to see.","created":"2026-08-25T15:02:03-04:00"}
{"id":"broken-file-blocks-healthy-writes","parent":"bug-mcp","ord":"a1","title":"One broken file refuses MCP writes to every healthy file","todo":true,"desc":"Observed 2026-08-25: while `orchestrator/lanes.olai` was failing typed validation, an `add_node` into the perfectly healthy `roadmap/bugs.olai` was refused with \"would leave the outlines invalid, so nothing was written\" — the write gate judges the whole set, so one broken file freezes the vault's entire write surface. Filing THIS VERY BUG was blocked by it.\n\nReads degrade gracefully per file (last-good); writes hard-refuse globally. The asymmetry is the question: a write that touches only healthy files arguably should go through, with the broken file's brokenness reported beside the success rather than in place of it. At minimum the refusal should NAME the broken file — \"would leave the outlines invalid\" reads as an indictment of the write, and the write was innocent.","created":"2026-08-25T15:02:14-04:00"}
{"id":"stale-set-reads-clean-writes-refuse","parent":"bug-server","ord":"aA","title":"A git rebase's file replacement goes unseen — reads serve the stale set with no error while writes refuse","todo":true,"desc":"Observed 2026-08-25: after `git pull --rebase` replaced the roadmap files on disk (the #395 migration landing), the running server kept serving the PRE-rebase content for 30+ minutes. `read_node`/`read_subtree` answered normally — no error, no staleness marker; the only symptom was every MCP write refusing (\"would leave the outlines invalid\", because the stale loaded set violated the new declarations). It took a disk-vs-MCP diff of one node (`master-red-meta-merge`: disk `agent-grok`, MCP `\"grok\"`) to prove the reads were stale.\n\nNeither `touch` nor a real content append (a trailing newline) woke it, and the 60s poll backstop that watcher-postboot-blind counts on never caught it either. Only a full restart resynced — which then hit the cold-boot bug filed beside this one.\n\nA stale set that reads healthily is worse than one that errors: every diagnosis trusts the reads. If the loaded set and disk can diverge, reads should say so.","see":["watcher-postboot-blind"],"created":"2026-08-25T15:02:23-04:00"}
{"id":"cold-boot-all-or-nothing","parent":"bug-server","ord":"aB","title":"Cold boot is all-or-nothing: two long-dangling `see` edges took the whole vault down, chat included","todo":true,"desc":"Observed 2026-08-25 at the post-#395 restart: `roadmap/features.olai` carried two dangling `see` edges (`md-live-preview-editor`, `capture-files` — targets removed days ago; the RUNNING server had served the file without complaint the whole time). At cold start there is no last-good to fall back to, so those two rows meant NOTHING was served — every page, MCP, and the chat panel (the human could not even reach the agent to report it; they had to `mv docs/* docs.stash/` to get a working app).\n\nTwo asymmetries, either's fix would have prevented the outage:\n1. **Running vs cold**: a running server survives a broken file (per-file last-good + catch-up); a cold one serves nothing until the whole set is perfect. The same two edges = silent for days, then total outage at restart.\n2. **Severity**: a dangling `see` is the same class as a dangling `after` or a stale ref-prop — a flag with a did-you-mean, not a brick. If it MUST be fatal at boot, it must be loud while the server still runs (\"this vault will not boot\"), because restart time is the worst moment to learn it.\n\nRecovery path that worked: fix the two edges in the stash, restore, let the watcher catch up.","see":["last-good-banner-flood"],"created":"2026-08-25T15:02:34-04:00"}
{"id":"typed-chips-doors","parent":"bug-outline","ord":"a4","title":"Typed properties × chip doors: values that name things the app can open draw no door, or the wrong face","todo":true,"desc":"`props/door.ts` predates #395 and never consults `_olai/Properties.olai` — doorhood is re-derived per value by shape-guessing (\"the whole value, exactly, or nothing\"), while the vault now DECLARES what values are. Where the guess and the declaration agree (URLs → away, dates → day, ref ids → node-in-set), chips work; the children are where they don't. The common fix is one consult: the declaration is the license the door module is currently missing.\n\nWhat works today, verified in code: `pr-url`/`merge-commit` (rule 1, whole URL), all five date keys (rule 2, day door + badge), ref/node ids (rule 3 — `namesFor` resolves against the whole derived set, cross-file).","created":"2026-08-25T15:41:26-04:00","custom":{"from":"the human 2026-08-25, sighting the brainstorm chip drawn link-colored but inert; the orchestrator's audit of props/door.ts against the typed vocabulary"}}
{"id":"prop-path-resolves-beside-writer","parent":"typed-chips-doors","ord":"a0","title":"Every `brief` and `brainstorm` chip outside the root is dead: paths resolve beside the WRITING file, the board writes vault-root-relative","todo":true,"desc":"The sighting: `brainstorm brainstorming/instructions-breakdown.md` on a roadmap/features.olai node draws link-colored and inert. door.ts rule 4 resolves a path value beside the file it was written in (Vault.from — \"exactly as a note's relative markdown link\"), so the value resolves to roadmap/brainstorming/… which the directory does not serve → not a path, stays text. The board's convention has always been vault-root-relative (`brief: briefs/tp.md` written in roadmap/*.olai and lanes.olai — ~101 uses, every one a dead chip). Fix shape: a key DECLARED `doc`/`path` resolves from the VAULT ROOT (the declaration is the stated convention), or both resolutions are tried, root first. The markdown-link analogy misfires here: a note's link is written by someone standing in a file; a board prop is written by a convention that stands at the root.","created":"2026-08-25T15:41:26-04:00"}
{"id":"ref-chip-face-shows-id","parent":"typed-chips-doors","ord":"a1","title":"A ref chip's face draws the raw variant id — the title resolves only on hover","todo":true,"desc":"The typed spec's display rule (Converged §1: values are IDS, \"display resolves it to the title — names rename, ids don't\") is half-drawn: the door exists (rule 3, id-in-set) and `door.says` carries the resolved title, but says lands only in `title=` (the hover tooltip, PropsDrawer.tsx:572/588) while the chip face draws the stored value verbatim. So every lane chip reads `agent agent-claude-opus`, `merge merge-auto` — the id prefix noise on every face, when the face should read `agent claude-opus…` (the target's title) with the id as the stored truth underneath.","created":"2026-08-25T15:41:26-04:00"}
{"id":"door-ignores-declarations","parent":"typed-chips-doors","ord":"a2","title":"door.ts never reads Properties.olai — the type that promises \"names a served document\" adds nothing to the display","todo":true,"desc":"The structural half of both siblings: doorhood is five shape-guesses per value, decided before #395 gave the vault a declared answer to the same question. A `doc`-typed value IS a served document by declaration (the gate refused it otherwise); a `path`-typed `worktree` deliberately is NOT a door (points anywhere, may not exist here); a `ref`-typed value names its variant. One consult of the declarations map in the door module grounds all of it — and keeps the wrong-door-is-worse-than-no-door rule, since the declaration is a stronger warrant than any shape guess. Undeclared keys keep today's guessing, so nothing regresses.","created":"2026-08-25T15:41:26-04:00"}
{"id":"cross-file-nudge-sentence","parent":"bug-nav","ord":"a6","title":"A cross-outline picker move that re-opens a done ancestor has nowhere on the page to draw the sentence","todo":true,"desc":"moving.tsx anchors the re-opened-done sentence to the moved row or its new parent — after a crossing, neither is on the page the person is standing on, so the one who caused the reopening never sees it. Where that sentence belongs (a toast? the destination's page on next visit? the picker's own confirmation?) is a UI decision. Related by shape: cross-pane drag still refuses cross-file (⌘⇧M is the door), which is the same which-page-owns-the-feedback question.","created":"2026-08-25T17:57:29-04:00","custom":{"from":"#400's author, 2026-08-25 — flagged in the PR body rather than guessed; the write itself is NOT silent (mark off, nudge on the answer, named in the commit subject); display-only, verified by the reviewer (axis 4)"}}
